\section{Introduction\label{intro}}

Isolated molecular systems do not interact with their environment and are thus
unobservable. In order to have a physical effect and be observed they must
respond to external probes, such as electromagnetic fields, and form a
composite interacting system whose evolution is described by a joint
Hamiltonian. The effect of radiation on the molecular subsystem can be
monitored by changes in the molecules density and reduced density matrices. If
the radiation is weak the behavior of the interaction is well described by the
linear response of the molecular system to the radiation, i.e. is linearly
proportional to the radiation strength, while if the radiation is intense the
molecular system responds in a nonlinear way. The interaction of the
electromagnetic field with molecules can be approximated in a standard semi
classical fashion by averaging over the photonic degrees of freedom to produce
effective one electron observables. This leads to a reduced Hamiltonian for
the molecular subsystem that includes perturbing effective time dependent one
body potentials which describe the interaction with the radiation field. In
the case of weak intensity radiation, where the linear response regime is
sufficient to describe the changes in the molecular system, one needs to only
consider responses that are first order in these observables.

The temporal behavior of a state of physical system is completely described by
the evolution of its full density matrix, which in term determines the
evolution of all of its reduced density matrices. These variations are
conveniently expressed in terms of various propagators, which thus provide a
comphrehensive formulation of the response of the molecular subsystem to
external probes. As is well known exact solutions for quantum evolutions have
only been determined for simple model systems, and are not known for
multi-electron molecular systems that involve coulombic interactions between
electrons. Thus for practical applications the goal is to develop systematic,
cost effective, theoretically well founded and non arbitrary approximation
schemes, that are accurate enough to explain experimental results. A major
focus of the illustrious career of professor Osvaldo Goscinski has been the
analysis and construction of approximate propagators, that fulfill these
objectives. In particular the one-electron and polarization propagators, where
he effectively used the properties of super operators acting on operator
manifolds associated with a given reference state to generate various orders
of approximation \cite{Goscinski73}\cite{Pickup73}\cite{Goscinski69}%
\cite{Goscinski70}\cite{Weiner80a}\cite{Goscinski80}\cite{Howat77a}. In this
article I will concentrate, for illustrative purposes, on the polarization
propagator for a molecular system with a fixed even number of electrons at a
fixed geometry. This propagator describes the linear response of the First
Order Reduced Density Matrix (FORDM) to electromagnetic radiation.

The polarization propagator is a hermitian matrix of time dependent response
functions of the matrix elements of the FORDM. If one takes the Laplace
transform of this propagator and expresses the resulting matrix in diagonal
form, in terms of normal mode operators, one can clearly observe that the
poles of this propagator are excitation energies and the associated residues
the transition probabilities for excitations generated by one particle
operators between stationary states, $\left\{  \left\vert \Psi_{k}%
\right\rangle \right\}  ,$ of the molecular system. If the initial molecular
reference state is stationary (usually the ground state, but not necessarily
so) then the poles are the excitation energies and the residues the transition
probabilities from that state. If the transition probability is 100\% for a
given one particle operator then this operator produces an excited state from
the initial stationary state and is called a state excitation operator. If
such an operator produces the excited state $\left\vert \Psi_{k}\right\rangle
,$ then its effect on the reference state $\left\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle $
is the same as the $N$-particle bra-ket operator $\left\vert \Psi
_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{0}\right\vert .$

Approximations to the polarization propagator are often based on the three
criteria, that

\begin{itemize}
\item all one particle operators that diagonalize the propagator matrix are
state excitation operators,

\item their adjoints annihilate the reference state,

\item the reference state is stationary with respect to at least one particle variations.
\end{itemize}

If an approximation satisfies these conditions one says that it is a
consistent approximation, however, in general, usually one or more of these
conditions are not satisfied and the approximation is inconsistent. Some
approximations can even lead to propagators that do not correspond to a valid
$N$-particle reference state and thus are not even $N$-representable.

An important point of note is that it is not necessary that the normal mode
one-particle operators actually produce excited states from the reference
state, in fact, in the exact case they generally do not. Hence there is thus
some level of ambiguity about polarization propagator approximations, if one
is trying to obtain an approximation to a one-particle linear response
function then the annihilation condition is not necessary, though stationarity
and $N$-representability are desirable, while if one is asserting that the
polarization propagator is a one-particle approximation to the full
$N$-particle propagator and is used to produce excited states, as well as
excitation energies and transition probabilities, then the annihilation
condition is necessary for consistency. During the course of this article
concrete examples of these points will be presented.

In the late 1970's it was found \cite{Weiner80}\cite{Weiner80a}%
\cite{Goscinski80} \cite{Linderberg77}\cite{Ohrn79} that an Antisymmetrized
Geminal Power (AGP) State was the most general $2M$ electron state that was
annihilated by a maximal set of one electron operators, (for odd number of
electrons Generalized AGP (GAGP) states \cite{GAGP} are needed but their
properties can be derived from AGP states). The $2M$-electron AGP $\left\vert
g^{M}\right\rangle $ state is an antisymmetrized product of $M$ two particle
geminals
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j<k\leq s}g_{jk}\left\vert \varphi
_{j}\varphi_{k}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  g_{ij}\right\}  $ are complex coefficients and $\left\{
\left.  \left\vert \varphi_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r=2s\right\}  $ is an orthonormal basis for the one particle Hilbert space
$\mathcal{H}^{1}.$ However even when the reference state was an energy
optimized AGP state the polarization propagator approximation failed to be
consistent, although the numerical results obtained from the application of
this approximation scheme agreed well with experimental values of excitation
energies and dipole induced transition probabilities i.e. oscillator
strengths\cite{Sangfelt85}\cite{Sangfelt84}\cite{Elander83}\cite{Goscinski82}%
\cite{Weiner83}\cite{Sangfelt81} \cite{Weiner87}\cite{Sangfelt87}%
\cite{Ohrn85}\cite{Weiner85}\cite{Weiner84a}\cite{Jensen82}\cite{Jorgen83}

It turns out that the annihilation condition can be naturally expressed in
terms of group Coherent States (CS) \cite{Perelomov_book}\cite{Klauder}%
\cite{Gilmore_book}, where the reference state corresponds to a lowest or
highest weight state associated with a representation of the group. The basic
ingredients needed for the construction of a family of group CS's in a vector
space is the existence of an irreducible representation of a group and a one
dimensional representation of a subgroup. The subgroup is called the isotropy
or invariance group of the CS's and the family of CS's is generated by the
action of the coset formed by factoring the group with respect to (wrt) this
subgroup. In the study of electronic systems it is natural to consider the
real, compact, \cite{Encyclo} Lie group $U(r)$ of unitary transformations of
the one electron state space $\mathcal{H}^{1}$ (of dimension $r=2s)$, that has
irreducible representations on all $N$-electron state spaces $\mathcal{H}^{N}%
$. Hence in this article we will primarily consider nonlinear manifolds of the
$N$-electron Hilbert space, $\mathcal{H}^{N},$ that are produced by families
of coherent states generated by cosets formed by one-particle operators. We
will construct approximate propagators based on stationary states of the
restriction of the Time Dependent Schr\"{o}dinger Equation (TDSE) to these
manifolds. Solutions to such restrictions of the TDSE are obtained by using
the Time Dependent Variational Principle (TDVP) \cite{Kramer81} or
equivalently the Stationary Phase Approximation (SPA) to the path integral
expressed in terms of paths on this CS manifold. The resulting Equations of
Motion (EOM)\ are those of a generalized \emph{classical }system described by
a curved phase space, a consequence of the fact that a family of group
coherent states forms a differentiable manifold \cite{Helgason78} whose
tangent spaces have an intrinsic symplectic structure \cite{Encyclo}.
Approximate stationary states of the molecular system then correspond to the
special stationary phase "paths" that are critical points of the corresponding
classical Hamiltonian. The linear response of these approximate stationary
states to external perturbing fields then involves the generators of the Lie
algebra of the group, and hence describes the response of the reduced density
matrix formed by these operators. Thus the response of the FORDM\ is
determined by generators of one-particle groups.

The path integral construction is powerful method that is often used to
evaluate the full quantum propagator between an initial and a final state. In
the CS approach path integrals can be formulated in terms of complex valued
paths in spaces of group coherent states. These spaces are complex
differentiable manifolds, that are also coset spaces of groups. The family of
coherent states chosen determines the specific geometric structure of the
space of the complex valued paths, $\mathrm{z}(t)$, via a metric function
$C\left(  \mathrm{z}(t),\mathrm{z}^{\prime}(t)\right)  $. The time development
of the paths is described by a classical Hamiltonian function $\mathcal{E}%
\left(  \mathrm{z},\mathrm{z}^{\prime},t\right)  $. The form and degree of
these functions is determined by the family of coherent states, and thus the
group-isotropy subgroup pair. If the Lagrangian associated with the
Hamiltonian function is quadratic then the path integral can be evaluated
exactly, if this is not the case approximations must be invoked.

In detail this article will be composed of the following sections. In section
\ref{liealg} we discuss the Lie algebra structure of Fermi-Fock space, which
is the direct sum of all of the state spaces $\left\{  \mathcal{H}%
^{N}\right\}  $, section \ref{one particle groups} will describe the possible
decompositions of the Lie algebra $su\left(  2s\right)  $ of $SU(2s)$, the one
particle group associated with $\mathcal{H}^{1},$ and thus the factorizations
of $SU(2s)$ that lead to manifolds of coherent states in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$
that can be based on $SU(2s)$. In section \ref{cohstate} we study in detail
the form of coherent states, in particular the family of AGP\ states, and the
geometric and phase space structure of coherent state manifolds. In section
\ref{pathinteg} we briefly describe the coherent state formulation of the path
integral and the SPA. In section \ref{linres} we come to the central topic of
this paper; Linear Response Theory in the context of coherent states. We
conclude with a discussion in section \ref{discussion}.

\section{Lie Algebras\label{liealg}}

The most convenient representation of the generators of the Lie algebra of
$SU(2s)$ for fermionic quantum mechanics is by second quantized operators
acting in Fermi-Fock space, $\mathcal{H}_{F}$, which is defined to be the
direct sum
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{F}=%
%TCIMACRO{\tbigoplus \limits_{0\leq N\leq r}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\bigoplus\limits_{0\leq N\leq r}}
%EndExpansion
\mathcal{H}^{N}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}^{N}=%
%TCIMACRO{\tbigwedge }%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\bigwedge}
%EndExpansion
\mathcal{H}^{1}%
\end{equation}
is the $N$ -fold antisymmetric tensor product of the one particle state space
$\mathcal{H}^{1}$, a space generated by an orthonormal basis of one particle
functions of position and spin
\begin{equation}
\left\{  \left.  \varphi_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\}  .
\end{equation}
The Banach space \cite{banach} of bounded operators acting in $\mathcal{H}%
_{F}$ is denoted by $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  ,$ and is
spanned by a second quantized operator basis
\begin{equation}
\left\{  \left.  a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{q}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots j_{p}\leq r;1\leq k_{1}<\cdots
k_{p}\leq r;1\leq p,q\leq r\right\}
\end{equation}
where the second quantized operators $\left\{  a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\}
$ act on the zero particle vacuum $\left\vert \phi\right\rangle $ in the
following fashion%
\begin{align}
a_{j}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle  &  =0\nonumber\\
a_{j}^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle  &  =\left\vert \varphi
_{j}\right\rangle
\end{align}
and satisfy the Canonical Anti-commutation Relationships (CAR)
\begin{equation}
\left[  a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{k}\right]  _{+}=\delta_{jk}I
\end{equation}
The operator space $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  $ contains
realizations of many classical Lie groups, algebras and universal enveloping
algebras \cite{envelop}. In particular the generators
\begin{equation}
\left\{  a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{k},a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}-\frac{1}{2}\delta
_{jk}I,a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger},a_{j}a_{k};1\leq j\leq k\leq r\right\}
\end{equation}
satisfy the commutation relationships of the lie algebra $so\left(
2r+1,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,$ (see \cite{gilmore} for example for the definitions
of classical lie groups and algebras), and generate a representation of the
universal enveloping algebra $so\left(  2r+1,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ in
$\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  ,$ which is fact all of
$\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  .$ The subset of these generators
\begin{equation}
\left\{  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}-\frac{1}{2}\delta_{jk}I,a_{j}^{\dagger}%
a_{k}^{\dagger},a_{j}a_{k};1\leq j\leq k\leq r\right\}
\end{equation}
satisfy the commutation relationships of the lie algebra of $so\left(
2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ and generate a representation of the universal
enveloping algebra of $so\left(  2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ in $\mathcal{B}%
\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  .$ The subset of these generators
\begin{equation}
\left\{  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}-\frac{1}{2}\delta_{jk}I;1\leq j\leq k\leq
r\right\}
\end{equation}
satisfy the commutation relationships of the lie algebra $su\left(  r\right)
$ and generate a representation of the universal enveloping algebra of
$su\left(  r\right)  $ in $\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}_{F}\right)  $. The
representation of $so\left(  2r+1,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ is irreducible, while
the representations of $so\left(  2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ and $su\left(
r\right)  $ are reducible. The irreducible representations of $so\left(
2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ are obtained by restricting to the subspace of even or
odd number states in $\mathcal{H}_{F},$ while the irreducible representations
of $su\left(  r\right)  $ are obtained by restricting to subspaces of states
of fixed integer particle number.

\section{One Particle Groups \label{one particle groups}}

In this section we describe the possible direct sum decompositions of the lie
algebra, $u(2s),$ of the group $U(2s)$ of unitary transformations of one
particle state space, $\mathcal{H}^{1}$ of dimension $2s,$ which lead in a
canonical fashion to families of CS's in $N$-particle state space
$\mathcal{H}^{N}$. These decompositions are based on the subgroup of special
orthogonal transformations, $SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,$ of a real
$2s$-dimensional Hilbert space, the subgroup, $U\left(  N\right)  \times
U\left(  2s-N\right)  $, formed by products of unitary transformations of
complex $N$ and $2s-N$ dimensional Hilbert spaces and the subgroup,
$USp\left(  2s\right)  $, of transformations of a complex $2s$-dimensional
Hilbert space, that are simultaneously unitary and symplectic. This latter
subgroup is isomorphic to the group of unitary transformations, $U\left(
s,\mathbb{Q}\right)  $ of a quaternionic $s$-dimensional Hilbert space. We
signify the CS's that they induce as $G/K$-CS's, where $G=SU\left(  2s\right)
$ and the subgroup $K\in\left\{  SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,SU\left(
N\right)  \times SU\left(  2s-N\right)  ,USp\left(  2s\right)  \right\}  $.

The Lie algebra $u(2s)$ has three direct sum Cartan decompositions
\cite{cartan} which are in terms of the Lie algebras, $so\left(
2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,$ $\left[  u\left(  N\right)  \oplus u\left(
2s-N\right)  \right]  ,$ and $usp\left(  2s\right)  ,$ of its maximal compact
(Appendix \ref{KillingForm}) subgroups $SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,$
$\left[  U\left(  N\right)  \times U\left(  2s-N\right)  \right]  ,$ and
$USp\left(  2s\right)  $, (see \cite{gilmore} for the definitions of these
groups). These decompositions are%
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
u\left(  2s\right)   &  =so\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  \oplus so\left(
2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ^{\bot}\label{d1}\\
u\left(  2s\right)   &  =\left[  u\left(  N\right)  \oplus u\left(
2s-N\right)  \right]  \oplus\left[  u\left(  N\right)  \oplus u\left(
2s-N\right)  \right]  ^{\bot};\quad0\leq N\leq2s\label{d2}\\
u\left(  2s\right)   &  =usp\left(  2s\right)  \oplus usp\left(  2s\right)
^{\bot} \label{d3}%
\end{align}
where the orthogonality is with respect to the killing form inner product
\cite{killing} (also see Appendix \ref{KillingForm}) of $u(2s)$. The
decomposition eqs. $\left(  \ref{d1},\ref{d2},\ref{d3}\right)  $ define the
following distinct coset decompositions of $U(2s)$%
\end{subequations}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
&  U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)
%}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  }%
%EndExpansion
;\quad0\leq N\leq2s\label{c1}\\
&  U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{USp\left(  2s\right)  }}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{USp\left(  2s\right)  }%
%EndExpansion
\label{c2}\\
&  U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{SO\left(  2s,\QTR{Bbb}{R}\right)  } }%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  }
%EndExpansion
\label{c3}%
\end{align}
and thus three classes of families of coherent states (see Section
\ref{cohstate}).

The motivation of using \emph{maximal} compact subgroups in eqs. $\left(
\ref{c1}\right)  ,\left(  \ref{c2}\right)  $ and $\left(  \ref{c3}\right)  $
is to produce coset spaces that are irreducible Riemannian Manifolds (a
Riemannian Manifold has tangent spaces that are inner product spaces
\cite{Riemannian}) i.e. they cannot be factored into a Riemannian product
\cite{Riemannian} of smaller Riemannian Manifolds. This technical feature,
although important in the classification of Riemannian Manifolds, is too
restrictive when considering manifolds of CS's, where we also need to examine
decompositions generated by subgroups of these maximal compact groups. We thus
enlarge the designation $G/K$-CS to include subgroups of $K$.

In order for any of the subgroups $\left\{  SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)
,\left\{  \left.  U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  \right\vert
0\leq N\leq2s\right\}  ,USp\left(  2s\right)  \right\}  ,$ or their subgroups,
to be isotropy groups of a given $N$-electron state, the state must carry a
one dimensional unitary representation of that subgroup and not of any larger
subgroup containing that subgroup.

There are \emph{no} states in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ that produce a one-dimensional
representation of $SO\left(  2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ and we conjecture (see
Appendix \ref{conjecture}) that there are \emph{no }subgroups of $SO\left(
2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  ,$ which \emph{are not} subgroups of $U\left(  N\right)
\otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  $ or $USp\left(  2s\right)  ,$ that produce
one-dimensional representations on $\mathcal{H}^{N}$. Thus there are \emph{no
}CS manifolds in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ based on subgroups of $SO\left(
2s,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ that cannot be described in terms of $U\left(
N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  $ or $USp\left(  2s\right)  $ CS's.

The only subgroup from the set $\left\{  \left.  U\left(  N\right)  \otimes
U\left(  2s-N\right)  \right\vert 0\leq N\leq2s\right\}  $ that produces a
one-dimensional representations in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ is $U\left(  N\right)
\otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  .$ The%
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)
%}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  }%
%EndExpansion
\end{equation}
-coherent states give the well known Thouless parameterization \cite{thouless}
of the manifold of Independent Particle States (IPS) and lead to the standard
Random Phase Approximation (RPA) for the linear response function. The
subgroup $U\left(  N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  $ is actually a
subgroup of $USp\left(  2s\right)  $ when $N=2M$ and we display in section
$\left(  \ref{cohstate}\right)  $ that $U\left(  2s\right)  /\left[  U\left(
N\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-N\right)  \right]  $-CS's can in fact be
described in terms of the class of $U\left(  2s\right)  /USp\left(  2s\right)
$-CS's.

We show by construction in section $\left(  \ref{cohstate}\right)  $ that the
group $USp\left(  2s\right)  $ and its subgroups do have one dimensional
representations in $\mathcal{H}^{N}=\mathcal{H}^{2M}$ and one can form
\begin{equation}
U\left(  2s\right)  /K-\text{coherent states,where }USp\left(  2s\right)
\supseteq K, \label{USPCS}%
\end{equation}
which are manifolds of general Antisymmetrized Geminal Power (AGP) states (see
Appendix \ref{AGP}). These states produce a generalized RPA for the linear
response function (see Section \ref{linres}). All manifolds of CS's in
$\mathcal{H}^{2M}$ based on $U\left(  2s\right)  $ can thus be expressed in
terms of the cosets eq. $\left(  \ref{USPCS}\right)  $ which have the form
\begin{equation}
U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{\left[  USp\left(  2\omega_{1}\right)  \cdots\times
%USp\left(  2\omega_{p}\right)  \times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}\times
%SU\left(  2\xi\right)  \right]  }}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{\left[  USp\left(  2\omega_{1}\right)  \cdots\times
USp\left(  2\omega_{p}\right)  \times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}\times
SU\left(  2\xi\right)  \right]  }%
%EndExpansion
,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}=\,\overset{\sigma-\text{factors}}{\overbrace
{SU\left(  2\right)  \times\cdots\times SU\left(  2\right)  }},
\end{equation}
the index set $\left\{  \sigma,\omega_{1},\cdots,\omega_{\rho},\xi\right\}  $
characterizes the type of AGP\ reference state and its elements satisfy
\begin{align}
2\sigma+2\omega+2\xi &  =r=2s\nonumber\\
\omega &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\rho}\omega_{j}.
\end{align}
The index set $\left\{  \sigma,\omega_{1},\cdots,\omega_{\rho},\xi\right\}  $
is determined by the array $\left\{  \left.  c_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
s\right\}  $ of canonical canonical coefficients \cite{weiner2003} of the
geminal that determines the AGP\ state according to:

\begin{description}
\item
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma=$ number of non zero, non equal $\left\{  c_{j}\right\}  $'s

\item $\omega_{j}=$ number of equal coefficients in the $j^{th}$ set

\item $\rho=$ number of sets of equal $\left\{  c_{j}\right\}  $'s

\item $\xi=$ number of zero coefficients.
\end{itemize}
\end{description}

If $\xi=0$ and $\omega=0\Leftrightarrow\rho=0,$ i.e. all the coefficients are
different and non zero, then the isotropy subgroup is
\begin{equation}
SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}=\overset{s-\text{factors}}{\overbrace{SU\left(
2\right)  \times\cdots\times SU\left(  2\right)  }}%
\end{equation}
which is the smallest one possible, leading to the biggest coset.\ In this
case the action of coset on the reference AGP generates the manifold of all
AGP\ states. If $\xi\neq0$ the reference AGP\ is produced by a degenerate
geminal and the action of the associated coset only produces a submanifold of
AGP\ states, all of which correspond to geminals that have the same null space
$\left(  \text{see Appendix \ref{AGP}}\right)  $ as the reference AGP. If
$\rho\neq0$ then the geminal has some extreme components and again the coset
only generates a sub manifold of AGP's from the reference. If $\sigma=0$,
$\xi=0$ and $\rho=s$ then the reference state is an extreme AGP\ (see Appendix
\ref{AGP}) and the action of the coset on the reference produces the manifold
of \emph{all }extreme AGP states.

All CS manifolds in $\mathcal{H}^{2M}$ based on the one particle group
$U\left(  2s\right)  $ are families of AGP\ states, some of these manifolds
are irreducible Riemannian manifolds and correspond to cosets formed by the
maximal compact subgroups $U\left(  2M\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-2M\right)
$ and $USp\left(  2s\right)  ,$ while others are reducible and correspond to
non maximal compact subgroups $USp\left(  2\omega_{1}\right)  \cdots\times
USp\left(  2\omega_{p}\right)  \times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}\times
SU\left(  2\xi\right)  $. Each of these manifolds of CS's have certain basic
physical properties e.g. $U\left(  2M\right)  \otimes U\left(  2s-2M\right)  $
invariant manifold describes uncorrelated Independent Particle States (IPS's),
the $USp\left(  2s\right)  $ invariant manifold describes highly correlated
extreme AGP\ states that are superconducting, while the $USp\left(
2\omega_{1}\right)  \cdots\times USp\left(  2\omega_{\rho}\right)  \times
SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}\times SU\left(  2\xi\right)  $ invariant
manifold for general $\left\{  \omega_{1},\cdots,\omega_{\rho},\sigma
,\xi\right\}  $ describe intermediate types of correlation and linear response
properties, see for a particular example \cite{oscillstrength}, most of which
have not been explored in any depth.

\section{Coherent States\label{cohstate}}

\subsection{Definition and Construction\label{def}}

The coherent state construction is based on the Hilbert space of states
$\mathcal{H}$ that carries unitary irreducible representations $\mathcal{R}%
\left(  G\right)  $ of a compact group $G$ on $\mathcal{H}$ and a one
dimensional representation of a subgroup $K\subset G$. The groups $G$ we
consider are $U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)  $ and $U\left(  \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\right)  $ the groups associated with $N$-electron state space and
$1$-electron state space respectively. In the finite dimensional case these
groups are the classical matrix groups and $U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)
\equiv U\left(  \left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  \right)  $ and $U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{1}\right)  \equiv U\left(
r\right)  ,$ in general we will use the matric notation even though in an
exact treatment $r=\infty$ and one should be aware of possible infinite
dimensional pathologies. The irreducible representations we consider are
$\mathcal{R}\left(  U\left(  \left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  \right)  \right)  $ and $\mathcal{R}\left(  U\left(  r\right)
\right)  $ that are carried by $\mathcal{H}^{N},$ unless otherwise stated we
will not distinguish between the groups $G$ and $K$ and their representations
on $\mathcal{H}^{N}$.

A state $\left\vert \Phi\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}^{N}$ is a reference state
for a group $G$ if there is an isotropy subgroup $K$ that is contained in $G$
that has $1$-dimensional representation on $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ defined by
\begin{equation}
K=\left\{  \left.  g\right\vert \quad\mathcal{R}\left(  g\right)  \left\vert
\Phi\right\rangle =e^{i\theta\left(  g\right)  }\left\vert \Phi\right\rangle
;g\in G;\theta\left(  g\right)  \in\mathbb{R}\right\}  . \label{subgpcon}%
\end{equation}
If such isotropy groups exists $G$ can be factored as%
\begin{equation}
G=\left(  G/K\right)  K.
\end{equation}


\subsection{AGP\ Coherent States}

The group $U\left(  2s\right)  $ has subgroups that leave reference
$2M$-electron AGP\ states,$\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ invariant
up to an overall phase factor and thus satisfy eq. $\left(  \ref{subgpcon}%
\right)  $. AGP states are given \cite{weiner2003} by%
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}%
<\cdots<j_{\frac{N}{2}}\leq s}c_{j_{1}}\cdots c_{j_{\frac{N}{2}}}\left\vert
\eta_{j_{1}}\eta_{j_{1}+s}\cdots\eta_{j_{N}}\eta_{j_{N}+s}\right\rangle ,
\label{canexp}%
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  \left.  \left\vert \eta_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert 1\leq
j\leq r\right\}  $ is a complete orthonormal basis for $\mathcal{H}^{1},$
$\left\{  \left.  c_{j_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq s\right\}  $ are positive
real numbers called the canonical coefficients of the two electron geminal
$\left\vert g\right\rangle $ which can be expanded in canonical form as%
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq s}c_{j_{1}}\left\vert \eta
_{j}\eta_{j+s}\right\rangle . \label{gemcan}%
\end{equation}
The state $\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ can also be expressed in
terms of the coset $SO\left(  2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  /SU\left(  r\right)  $ of
the group $SO\left(  2r,\mathbb{R}\right)  $ acting on the zero particle
vacuum state $\left\vert \phi\right\rangle $ of $\mathcal{H}_{F}$
\cite{WeinerOhrnDeumens1}\cite{weinerOhrnDeumens2}\cite{Rowe} as
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =P_{\frac{N}{2}}\exp\left\{
\sum_{1\leq j\leq s}c_{j_{1}}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j+s}^{\dagger}\right\}
\left\vert \phi\right\rangle
\end{equation}
where $P_{\frac{N}{2}}$ is the orthogonal projector onto $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ and
$\left\{  \left.  b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq s\right\}  $ are the
creators for the one particle states $\left\{  \left.  \left\vert \eta
_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\}  $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
b_{j}^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\left\vert \eta_{j}\right\rangle
.
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Coset Factorization for $U\left(  2s\right)  $\label{factor}}

In general the action of $U\left(  2s\right)  $ on a reference AGP,
$\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $, state produces a manifold,
$\mathcal{M}\left(  \sigma,\omega_{1},\cdots,\omega_{\rho},\xi\right)  $ of
AGP states given by
\begin{equation}
\left\{  U\left(  2s\right)
%TCIMACRO{\QATOPD{/}{.}{{}}{K}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{/}{.}{0pt}{}{{}}{K}%
%EndExpansion
\times K\right\}  \left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle . \label{su2act}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
K=USp\left(  2\omega_{1}\right)  \cdots\times USp\left(  2\omega_{p}\right)
\times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma}\times SU\left(  2\xi\right)  .
\end{equation}
Each element of the manifold can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g\left(  c\right)  ^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =ck_{\omega_{1}%
}\cdots k_{\omega_{\rho}}k_{\sigma}k_{\xi}\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}%
}\right\rangle =c\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
where the isotropy subgroup is given by the products $k_{\omega_{1}}\cdots
k_{\omega_{\rho}}k_{\sigma}k_{\xi}$ where%
\begin{equation}
k_{\omega_{j}}\in USp\left(  2\omega_{j}\right)  ;1\leq j\leq\rho,k_{\sigma
}\in SU\left(  2\right)  ^{\sigma},k_{\xi}\in SU\left(  2\xi\right)  ,
\end{equation}
$c\in U\left(  2s\right)  /K$ and the reference AGP is generated by a geminal
$\left\vert g\right\rangle $ in the class $\left\{  \rho,\sigma,\xi\right\}
.$ The most general AGP manifold is produced when $\sigma=s$ ($\Leftrightarrow
\left(  \rho=0\text{ \& }\xi=0\right)  $ ) so it is advantages to first
consider that case.

\paragraph{$SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}$ factors}

The isotropy sub group, $K$, for an AGP reference state that has non equal and
nonzero canonical coefficients $\left\{  \left.  c_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
s\right\}  ,$ i.e. $\sigma=s,$ is the product group $SU\left(  2\right)
^{s},$ which produces the coset decomposition
\begin{equation}
U\left(  2s\right)  =\left[  U\left(  2s\right)  /SU\left(  2\right)
^{s}\right]  \times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}.
\end{equation}
One can express coset representatives in a factored form as the product
$G_{+1}G_{0}G_{-1}$, \cite{Gilmore} where $\left\{  G_{+1},G_{0}%
,G_{-1}\right\}  $ are subgroups of the linear group $GL\left(  2s,\mathbb{C}%
\right)  ,\ $which is the complexification of $U\left(  2s\right)  ,$ and the
group $G_{-1}$ leaves the reference AGP state invariant (note that it is
different to the real compact isotropy groups). Restricting attention to the
connected components of these subgroups, that contain the identity element,
one can express this factorization in terms of Lie algebras as
\begin{equation}
U\left(  2s\right)  =e^{N_{+}}e^{N_{0}}e^{N_{-}}e^{\mathcal{K}}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{K}=\,\overset{s\text{-subspaces}}{\overbrace{su\left(  2\right)
\oplus\cdots\oplus su\left(  2\right)  }},
\end{equation}
$\left\{  N_{+1},N_{0},N_{-1}\right\}  $ are Lie subalgebras of $gl\left(
2s,\mathbb{C}\right)  ,$ the Lie algebra of $GL\left(  2s,\mathbb{C}\right)
$. The subalgebras $\left\{  N_{-1},\mathcal{K}\right\}  $ annihilate
$\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
b\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =0\quad\forall b\in N_{-1}%
\oplus\mathcal{K},
\end{equation}
while the subalgebras $\left\{  N_{+1},N_{0}\right\}  $ have the property
\begin{equation}
b\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle \neq0\quad\forall b\in N_{+1}\oplus
N_{0}.
\end{equation}
Introducing coordinate systems $\left\{  \mathbf{z}_{+},\mathbf{\lambda,z}%
_{-},\mathbf{\eta}\right\}  $ with respect to fixed bases of $\left\{
N_{+1},N_{0},N_{-1},\mathcal{K}\right\}  ,$ that depend on the reference AGP
state, one can parameterize group elements $u\in U\left(  2s\right)  $ as
\begin{equation}
u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  =\tilde{c}\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z}%
}\right)  k\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  =c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)
c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)  c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{-}\right)
k\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  .
\end{equation}
The action of $U\left(  2s\right)  $ on the AGP state is
\begin{equation}
u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  \left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}%
}\right\rangle =\tilde{c}\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z}}\right)  k\left(
\mathbf{\eta}\right)  \left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =c_{+1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{+}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)  c_{-1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{-}\right)  k\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  \left\vert g^{\frac
{N}{2}}\right\rangle =c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)  c_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\lambda}\right)  \left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
showing that the non redundant coordinates $\mathbf{z}\equiv\left(
\mathbf{z}_{+},\mathbf{\lambda}\right)  $ describe a configuration space, that
is a sub manifold $\mathcal{M}$ (in general nonlinear) of $\mathcal{H}^{N}$
with a coordinate system $\left\{  \mathbf{z}\right\}  ,$ that parameterize
the set of $\left\{  \left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle
\right\}  $ of $U\left(  2s\right)  /SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}$-coherent
states
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle =c_{+1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{+}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)  \left\vert
g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle \equiv c\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \left\vert
g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
and at the same time represents the coset space $U\left(  2s\right)
/SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}$. The reference AGP is generated by a geminal
$\left\vert g\right\rangle $ in the class $\left\{  \rho,\sigma,\xi\right\}
=\left\{  0,s,0\right\}  $

The AGP\ state dependent bases one chooses are
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
N_{+1}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  c_{j}a_{k}^{\dagger
}a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  c_{k}a_{-j}^{\dagger
}a_{-k};\right. \nonumber\\
&  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad
\quad\quad\quad\left.  -s\leq j<k\leq s,\left\vert j\right\vert \neq\left\vert
k\right\vert ;\,\sigma\left(  j\right)  =\operatorname{sign}\left(  j\right)
\right\} \label{l1}\\
N_{0}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  \left(  a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}%
+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger
}a_{-j}\right)  ,\right. \nonumber\\
&  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad
\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\left.  i\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{-j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ;1\leq j\leq s\right\} \label{l2}\\
N_{-1}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  c_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger
}a_{j}+\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  c_{j}a_{-j}^{\dagger
}a_{-k}\right. \nonumber\\
&  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad
\quad\quad\quad\left.  -s\leq j<k\leq s,\left\vert j\right\vert \neq\left\vert
k\right\vert ;\,\sigma\left(  j\right)  =\operatorname{sign}\left(  j\right)
\right\} \label{l3}\\
\mathcal{K}  &  =\mathbb{R-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  i\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ,i\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}%
-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ;\quad1\leq j\leq s\right\}  , \label{l4}%
\end{align}
where we have translated the indexing of the CGSO's by
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \eta_{j}\right\rangle \rightarrow\left\vert \eta_{j-s}\right\rangle
\quad1\leq j\leq2s.
\end{equation}
It is useful to note that
\begin{equation}
N_{0}=\left\{  \mathcal{K\oplus N}\right\}  ^{\mathbb{C}}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{N=\,}%
%TCIMACRO{\tbigoplus _{1\leq j\leq s}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\bigoplus_{1\leq j\leq s}}
%EndExpansion
\left\{  \mathbb{R-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  \left.  \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  \right\vert \quad1\leq
j\leq s\right\}  \right\}  .
\end{equation}
and $\left\{  {}\right\}  ^{\mathbb{C}}$ denotes complexification. The factors
in the group decomposition are%
\begin{align}
c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)   &  =\exp\left\{  \sum
\limits_{\substack{-s\leq j<k\leq s\\\left\vert j\right\vert \neq\left\vert
k\right\vert }}z_{+jk}\left\{  c_{j}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}-\sigma\left(
j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  c_{k}a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}\right\}
\right\}  =\exp\sum\limits_{_{\substack{-s\leq j<k\leq s\\\left\vert
j\right\vert \neq\left\vert k\right\vert }}}z_{+jk}q_{jk}^{\dagger
};\label{co1}\\
c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{-}\right)   &  =\exp\left\{  \sum
\limits_{\substack{-s\leq j<k\leq s\\\left\vert j\right\vert \neq\left\vert
k\right\vert }}z_{-jk}\left\{  c_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}+\sigma\left(
j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  c_{j}a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}\right\}
\right\}  =\exp\sum\limits_{_{\substack{-s\leq j<k\leq s\\\left\vert
j\right\vert \neq\left\vert k\right\vert }}}z_{-jk}q_{jk}\label{co2}\\
c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)   &  =\exp\left\{  \sum
\limits_{_{\substack{1\leq j\leq s\\1\leq k\leq4}}}\lambda_{jk}n_{jk}\right\}
\label{co3}\\
h\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)   &  =\exp\left\{  \sum
\limits_{_{\substack{1\leq j\leq s\\1\leq k\leq3}}}\eta_{jk}u_{jk}\right\}
\label{co4}%
\end{align}
where $\left\{  z_{+jk},z_{-jk}\right\}  \in\mathbb{C},\left\{  \lambda
_{jk},\eta_{jk}\right\}  \in\mathbb{R}$ , and $\left\{  n_{jk},u_{jk}\right\}
$ are given by equ's. $\left(  \ref{l2}\right)  $ and $\left(  \ref{l4}%
\right)  $ respectively.

In order that the transformation $c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)
c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)  c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{-}\right)
k\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  $ be unitary the values of $\left\{
\mathbf{z}_{+},\mathbf{\lambda,z}_{-}\right\}  $ must be constrained resulting
in dependencies between the variables. The action of such unitary
transformations on a normalized reference state produce normalized coherent
states that can be expressed in terms of the coset representatives
$c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)  $.
However there are dependencies between the variables $\left\{  \mathbf{z}%
_{+}\right\}  $ and $\left\{  \mathbf{\lambda}\right\}  $ which are often
convenient to avoid. One method of achieving this is to consider unnormalized
coherent states by modifying the coset representative $c_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\lambda}\right)  $ and explicitly considering the norm of the state in
various expectation value expressions.

\subsubsection{$USp\left(  \omega_{j}\right)  $ factors}

The changes from the $SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}$ case can be illustrated by an
$\sigma=s-2,\rho=1,\omega_{1}=2$ example, which clearly leads to the general
picture for $\rho>1$. Here we have $s-2$ eigenvalues, $\left\{  \left.
c_{j}^{2}\right\vert 3\leq j\leq s\right\}  $ of $gg^{\dag}$ that are doubly
degenerate and one fourfold degenerate eigenvalue, $c_{1}^{2}$, of $gg^{\dag}%
$, which is associated with two doubly degenerate eigenvalues, $\left\{
c_{1},-c_{1}\right\}  $ of $g$ affiliated with CGSO's that are linear
combinations of $\left\{  \left\vert \eta_{1}\right\rangle ,\left\vert
\eta_{2}\right\rangle \right\}  $ and $\left\{  \left\vert \eta_{-1}%
\right\rangle ,\left\vert \eta_{-2}\right\rangle \right\}  $ respectively
$\left(  \cite{weiner2003}\right)  $. In this case unitary transformations,
$u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  $, belonging to $U\left(  2s\right)
$ can be expressed in factored form as
\begin{equation}
u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  =c\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z}}\right)
k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma}\right)  k_{\omega}\left(
\mathbf{\eta}_{\omega}\right)  =c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma+}\right)
u_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\omega+}\right)  u_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda
}_{\omega}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)
u_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\omega-}\right)  c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}%
_{-}\right)  k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma}\right)  k_{\omega
}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\omega}\right)  . \label{gpprod}%
\end{equation}
where $k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma}\right)  \in SU\left(
2\right)  ^{s-2}$ and $c_{\sigma0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)
\in\exp N_{0}$ and are expanded in the basis eqs.$\left(  \ref{l2}\right)  $
and $\left(  \ref{l4}\right)  $ respectively with the operators referring to
CGSO's $\left\{  \left.  \left\vert \eta_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert
j=\pm1,\pm2\right\}  $ deleted. The operators $c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}%
_{\sigma+}\right)  \in\exp N_{+1}$ and $c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma
-}\right)  \in\exp N_{-1}$ are expanded in the basis eqs.$\left(
\ref{l1}\right)  $ and $\left(  \ref{l3}\right)  $ with the operators
referring to the pairs $\left\{  \left.  \left(  \left\vert \eta
_{j}\right\rangle ,\left\vert \eta_{k}\right\rangle \right)  \right\vert
j=\pm1,k=\pm1\right\}  $ of CGSO's deleted. The factors $u_{+1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{\omega+}\right)  \in\exp U_{+11},u_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}%
_{\omega-}\right)  \in\exp U_{-11},u_{\omega0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}%
_{\omega}\right)  \in\exp U_{01},k_{\omega}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\omega
}\right)  \in USp\left(  4\right)  $, where $U_{+1},U_{0},U_{-1}$ and
$usp\left(  4\right)  $ are expanded in the following bases%

\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
U_{+11}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  a_{k}^{\dagger}%
a_{j}+\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-j}^{\dagger}%
a_{-k};\quad j=1,-1;k=2,-2\right\} \label{u1}\\
U_{01}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  \left(  a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}%
-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}+a_{-j}%
^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ,\right. \nonumber\\
&  i\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ,i\left(
a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)
a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}-a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}+\sigma\left(  j\right)
\sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-k}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\nonumber\\
&  \left.  \left(  a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(
k\right)  a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}+a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}-\sigma\left(  j\right)
\sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-k}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ;\quad
j=1,-1;k=2,-2\right\} \label{u2}\\
U_{-11}  &  =\mathbb{C-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  a_{k}^{\dagger}%
a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-j}^{\dagger}%
a_{-k};\quad j=1,-1;k=2,-2\right\} \label{u3}\\
usp\left(  2\omega_{1}\right)   &  =usp\left(  4\right)  =\mathbb{R-}%
\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  i\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger
}a_{-j}\right)  ,i\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}+a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)
,\left(  a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{-j}-a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)  ,\right.
\nonumber\\
&  \left(  a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(
k\right)  a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}-a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}+\sigma\left(  j\right)
\sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-k}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ,\nonumber\\
&  \left.  i\left(  a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}-\sigma\left(  j\right)  \sigma\left(
k\right)  a_{-j}^{\dagger}a_{-k}+a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}-\sigma\left(  j\right)
\sigma\left(  k\right)  a_{-k}^{\dagger}a_{-j}\right)  ;\quad
j=1,-1;k=2,-2\right\}  , \label{u4}%
\end{align}
where $\sigma\left(  j\right)  =\operatorname{sign}\left(  j\right)  .$ The
manifold of $U\left(  2s\right)  /\left[  USp\left(  4\right)  \times
SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s-1}\right]  $-coherent states is then given by
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle =c_{+1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{+\sigma}\right)  u_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+\omega}\right)
u_{01}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\omega_{1}}\right)  c_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)  \left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{z\equiv}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+\sigma},\mathbf{z}_{+\omega
},\mathbf{\lambda}_{\omega},\mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)  $ and the
reference AGP is generated by a geminal $\left\vert g\right\rangle $ in the
class $\left\{  \rho,\sigma,\xi\right\}  =\left\{  1,s-1,0\right\}  .$

If $\rho>2$ then one deletes the appropriate operators in the bases $\left\{
N_{+1},N_{0},N_{-1},\mathcal{K}\right\}  $ defined in eqs. $\left(
\ref{l1}\right)  -\left(  \ref{l4}\right)  $ and defines new bases $\left\{
\left.  \left\{  U_{+1j},U_{0j},U_{-1j},usp\left(  2\omega_{j}\right)
\right\}  \right\vert 1\leq j\leq\rho\right\}  $ according to eqs. $\left(
\ref{u1}\right)  -\left(  \ref{u4}\right)  .$ The subalgebras $\left\{
\left.  U_{-1j},usp\left(  2\omega_{j}\right)  \right\vert 1\leq j\leq
\rho\right\}  $ annihilate $\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
b\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =0\quad\forall b\in\bigoplus
\limits_{1\leq j\leq\rho}U_{-1j}\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq j\leq\rho
}usp\left(  2\omega_{j}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
while the subalgebras $\left\{  \left.  U_{+1j},U_{0j}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq\rho\right\}  $ have the property
\begin{equation}
b\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle \neq0\quad\forall b\in\bigoplus
\limits_{1\leq j\leq\rho}U_{+1j}\oplus\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq j\leq\rho}%
U_{0j}.
\end{equation}
The $U\left(  2s\right)  /\left[
%TCIMACRO{\tprod _{1\leq j\leq\rho}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\prod_{1\leq j\leq\rho}}
%EndExpansion
USp\left(  2\omega_{j}\right)  \times SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s-\omega}\right]
$-coherent state manifold is then given by
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle =c_{+1}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{\sigma+}\right)
%TCIMACRO{\tprod _{1\leq j\leq\rho}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\prod_{1\leq j\leq\rho}}
%EndExpansion
\left\{  u_{+1j}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+j}\right)  u_{0j}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda
}_{j}\right)  \right\}  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)
\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}


The manifold of extreme AGP's $\mathcal{M}\left(  0,s,0\right)  $ are
$U\left(  2s\right)  /USp\left(  2s\right)  $-coherent state, which have been
discussed in super conductivity \cite{Colemansuper} is given by
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle =u_{+11}\left(
\mathbf{z}_{+1}\right)  u_{01}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{1}\right)  \left\vert
g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{$SU\left(  2\xi\right)  $ factors}

The changes from $SU\left(  2\right)  ^{s}$ can this time be illustrated by an
$\sigma=s-1,\rho=0,\xi=1$ example, which also clearly leads to the general
picture for $\xi>1$. Here we have $s-1$ eigenvalues, $\left\{  \left.
c_{j}^{2}\right\vert 2\leq j\leq s\right\}  $ of $gg^{\dag}$ that are doubly
degenerate and a doubly degenerate zero eigenvalue of $gg^{\dag}$, which is
associated with the CGSO's $\left\{  \left\vert \eta_{-1}\right\rangle
,\left\vert \eta_{1}\right\rangle \right\}  .$ In this case unitary
transformations, $u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  $, belonging to
$U\left(  2s\right)  $ can be expressed in factored form as
\begin{equation}
u\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z},\eta}\right)  =c\left(  \mathbf{\tilde{z}}\right)
k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma}\right)  k_{\xi}\left(  \mathbf{\eta
}_{\xi}\right)  =c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma+}\right)  v_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\lambda}_{\xi}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)
c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{-}\right)  k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma
}\right)  k_{\xi}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\xi}\right)  , \label{untrans}%
\end{equation}
where $k_{\sigma}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}_{\sigma}\right)  \in SU\left(
2\right)  ^{s-1}$ and $c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)  \in\exp
N_{0}$ which are expanded in the basis eqs.$\left(  \ref{l2}\right)  $,
$\left(  \ref{l4}\right)  $ with the operators referring to CGSO's $\left\{
\left.  \left\vert \eta_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert j=\pm1\right\}  $
deleted. The definitions of $c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma+}\right)
\in\exp N_{+1}$ and $c_{-1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma-}\right)  \in\exp
N_{-1}$ are unchanged and are still expanded in the basis eqs.$\left(
\ref{l1}\right)  $ and $\left(  \ref{l3}\right)  $. The factors $v_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\lambda}_{\xi}\right)  \in\exp V_{0},k_{\xi}\left(  \mathbf{\eta}%
_{\xi}\right)  \in SU\left(  2\right)  $, where $su\left(  2\right)  $ is
expanded in the following bases%
\begin{equation}
su\left(  2\right)  =\mathbb{R-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  i\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{-1}+a_{-1}^{\dagger}a_{1}\right)  ,i\left(  a_{1}^{\dagger
}a_{1}-a_{-1}^{\dagger}a_{-1}\right)  ,\left(  a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{-1}%
-a_{-1}^{\dagger}a_{1}\right)  \right\}
\end{equation}
and $V_{0}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\left\{  su\left(  2\right)  \mathcal{\oplus\,N}\right\}  ^{\mathbb{C}}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{N}=\mathbb{R-}\operatorname{linspan}\left\{  \left(  a_{1}^{\dagger
}a_{1}+a_{-1}^{\dagger}a_{-1}\right)  \right\}  .
\end{equation}
The subalgebra $\left\{  su\left(  2\xi\right)  \right\}  $ annihilates
$\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
b\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle =0\quad\forall b\in su\left(
2\xi\right)  .
\end{equation}
As the class of the reference AGP\ changes from $\left(  s-1,0,1\right)  $
to$\left(  s-p,0,p\right)  $ it is clear how the definitions of the factors
eq. $\left(  \ref{untrans}\right)  $ evolve. It is easy to confirm that the
$\left(  M,0,s-M\right)  $ cannot in fact exist, while the class $\left(
0,M,s-M\right)  $ does and such reference AGP's are IPS's and the $U\left(
2s\right)  /\left[  USp\left(  2M\right)  \times SU\left(  2s-2M\right)
\right]  $-coherent state manifold is in fact the Thouless $U\left(
2s\right)  /\left[  U\left(  2M\right)  \times U\left(  2s-2M\right)  \right]
$-coherent state manifold.

As an illustration of the preceding forms of the cosets of $U\left(
\mathcal{H}^{1}\right)  $ we can consider their action on an AGP\ state
$\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle $ determined by a geminal
\begin{equation}
\left\vert g\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq s}c_{j_{1}}\left\vert \eta
_{j}\eta_{j+s}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
given by
\begin{equation}
c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+}\right)  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}\right)
c_{+1}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{\sigma+}\right)  v_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\xi
}\right)
%TCIMACRO{\tprod _{1\leq j\leq\rho}}%
%BeginExpansion
{\textstyle\prod_{1\leq j\leq\rho}}
%EndExpansion
\left\{  u_{+1j}\left(  \mathbf{z}_{+j}\right)  u_{0j}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda
}_{j}\right)  \right\}  c_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\lambda}_{\sigma}\right)
\left\vert g^{\frac{N}{2}}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
$\sigma=$ number of non zero, non equal $\left\{  c_{j}\right\}  $'s

\begin{description}
\item
\begin{itemize}
\item $\omega_{j}=$ number of equal coefficients in the $j^{th}$ set

\item $\rho=$ number of sets of equal $\left\{  c_{j}\right\}  $'s

\item $\xi=$ number of zero coefficients.
\end{itemize}
\end{description}

\subsection{$U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)  /U\left(  1\right)  $-Coherent
States}

In order to examine the properties of general state excitation operators in
terms of CS's it is necessary and illuminating to consider the \emph{exact
}quantum evolution equations expressed in terms of a CS manifold. The
resulting classical equations of motion (see Section \ref{SPA}) defined on
such a manifold are equivalent to the full TDSE. The configuration space
formed by these coherent sates represents all of the states $\left\vert
\Psi\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}^{N}$ via a parameterization $\left\{
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle \right\}  $. The group
$G$ in this case is the full unitary group $U\left(  \left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  \right)  $ of $N$-electron state space and the isotropy subgroup is
\begin{equation}
K=\left\{  \left.  g\right\vert g\in G,g\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle
=e^{i\theta\left(  g\right)  }\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle \right\}
=\left\{  \exp\left(  i\lambda\left(  g\right)  \left\vert \Phi_{1}%
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \exp\left(
i\sum_{i,j\neq1}\lambda_{ij}\left(  g\right)  \left\vert \Phi_{i}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{j}\right\vert \right)  \right\}
\end{equation}
The coset representatives are
\begin{equation}
G/K=\left\{  \exp\left(  \sum_{i>1}\eta_{i}\left\vert \Phi_{i}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \exp\left(  \eta_{1}\left\vert
\Phi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \exp\left(
-\sum_{i>1}\bar{\eta}_{i}\left\vert \Phi_{i}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \right\}
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  \left.  \left\vert \Phi_{i}\right\rangle \right\vert
\quad1\leq i\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  \right\}  $ is a complete orthonormal basis for $\mathcal{H}^{N}$.
The family of CS's associated with this coset is defined as
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  \right\rangle =\left\{
\exp\left(  \sum_{i>1}\eta_{i}\left\vert \Phi_{i}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \exp\left(  \eta_{1}\left\vert \Phi
_{i}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert \right)  \right\}
\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
It is convenient to introduce a new equivalent coordinate system $\left\{
\mathbf{z}\right\}  $ by setting
\begin{align}
\eta_{1}  &  =\ln\left(  z_{1}\right)  \Leftrightarrow z_{1}=e^{\eta_{1}}\\
\eta_{j}  &  =\frac{z_{j}}{z_{1}};\quad2\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  \Leftrightarrow z_{j}=\eta_{j}e^{\eta_{1}}%
\end{align}
which leads to the following form for the coherent states
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }z_{j}\left\vert \Phi_{j}\right\rangle \label{CI}%
\end{equation}
Clearly eq. $\left(  \ref{CI}\right)  $ is the familiar CI expansion of a
general $N$-fermion state and the parameters $\left\{  z_{j}\right\}  $ the
configurational interaction coefficients. The normalization of these CS's is
given by
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\left\vert z_{j}\right\vert ^{2}=\left\vert z_{1}\right\vert
^{2}+\sum_{2\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\left\vert z_{j}\right\vert ^{2}\\
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\eta}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(
\mathbf{\eta}\right)  \right\rangle  &  =e^{2\operatorname{Re}\eta_{1}%
}\left\{  1+\sum_{2\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\left\vert \eta_{j}\right\vert ^{2}\right\}
\end{align}
Hence if one sets $\eta_{1}$ as the function
\begin{equation}
\eta_{1}=\eta_{1}\left(  \eta_{2},\cdots,\eta_{\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\right)  =\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(  1-\sum_{2\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }e^{2\operatorname{Re}\eta_{j}}\right)  \label{n1}%
\end{equation}
and $z_{1}$ as the function%
\begin{equation}
z_{1}=z_{1}\left(  z_{2},\cdots,z_{\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\right)  =\left(  1-\sum_{2\leq j\leq\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\left\vert z_{j}\right\vert ^{2}\right)  ^{\frac{1}{2}} \label{n2}%
\end{equation}
the family of CS's $\left\{  \left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle \right\}  \equiv\left\{  \left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\eta
}\right)  \right\rangle \right\}  $ are normalized if the reference state
$\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle $ is normalized i.e. $\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{1}\right\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle =1.$ If one uses the CS parameters
$\left\{  \eta_{2},\cdots,\eta_{\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\right\}  $ or $\left\{  z_{2},\cdots,z_{\left(
%TCIMACRO{\QATOP{r}{N}}%
%BeginExpansion
\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r}{N}%
%EndExpansion
\right)  }\right\}  $ the CS manifold is the unit sphere in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$,
while if one uses $\left\{  \mathbf{\eta}\right\}  $ or $\left\{
\mathbf{z}\right\}  $ then it is all of $\mathcal{H}^{N}$ and one explicitly
considers the normalization in all expressions.

\section{Differential Structure of the Coherent State
Manifold\label{diffstruct}}

\subsection{General Structure}

In the following we gloss over technical, (but mathematical necessary),
qualifications and discuss some properties of the differentiable manifold,
$\mathcal{M}$ (also see Appendix \ref{manifold}) formed by the CS's. The
manifold of $G/K-$CS's has a hermitian structure \cite{Helgason} given by the
tensor
\begin{equation}
h\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  d\bar{z}_{j}\mathbf{\otimes}dz_{k},
\end{equation}
where the coefficients $\left\{  \left.  C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
,z}\right)  \right\vert 1\leq j,k\leq\nu\right\}  ,$ form a positive,
(therefore hermitian), metric matrix defined by
\begin{equation}
C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  =\frac{\partial^{2}\Lambda\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}\partial z_{k}},
\end{equation}
called a K\"{a}hler metric \cite{DeWitt, Helgason}, with a K\"{a}hler
potential $\Lambda\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  $ is given by%
\begin{equation}
\Lambda\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  =\ln S\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
,z}\right)  =\ln\left\langle \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  |\Phi\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle .
\end{equation}
The map
\begin{equation}
\Phi:\mathbb{C}^{\nu}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}%
\end{equation}
given by the preceding parameterization of the cosets is constructed to be a
diffeomorphism (a 1-1, continuous function that is differentiable) and is thus
the inverse of a coordinate chart \cite{Helgason}
\begin{equation}
\Phi^{-1}:\mathcal{M}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{\nu}.
\end{equation}
The coordinate functions
\begin{equation}
z_{j}:\mathcal{M}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
associated with this chart are
\begin{equation}
z_{j}\left(  m\right)  =\left(  \Phi^{-1}\right)  _{j}\left(  m\right)
,\qquad m\in\mathcal{M}.
\end{equation}
Points on the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ are $\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  ,$
which defines an association between $\mathcal{M}$ and $\mathbb{C}^{\nu}$
given by $\Phi$, however as we are in general only considering one
representative coordinate chart we will abuse the notation when the meaning is
unambiguous and the point $\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  $ will be denoted as
$\mathbf{z}$.

The differential one forms $\left\{  \left.  dz_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
\nu\right\}  $ are the best linear approximations to the coordinate functions
$\left\{  \left.  z_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\}  $ at the point
$\mathbf{z}\equiv\left(  z_{1},\cdots,z_{\nu}\right)  ,$ (as is standard the
$\mathbf{z}$ dependence of these forms is not explicitly shown) and thus are
complex valued linear functionals on the the tangent spaces $\left\{  \left.
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}\right\vert \mathbf{z\in}\mathcal{M}\right\}  $,
(which rigorously should be denoted as $\left\{  \left.  \mathcal{T}%
_{\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }\right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\mathbf{\in}\mathcal{M}\right\}  $), i.e.
\begin{equation}
dz_{j}:\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
and hence together with $\left\{  d\bar{z}_{j}\right\}  $ generate the dual
spaces, $\left\{  \mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}^{\ast}\right\}  $ of $\left\{
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}\right\}  $. The tangent spaces, $\left\{
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}\right\}  ,$ are the direct sum of the complex linear
space, $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}^{\left(  1,0\right)  }$, spanned by the
vectors $\left\{  \left.  \frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq\nu\right\}  ,$\emph{with} the the complex linear space, $\mathcal{T}%
_{\mathbf{z}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }$, spanned by the vectors $\left\{
\left.  \frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
\nu\right\}  ,$ (again the $\mathbf{z}$ dependence is not explicitly shown).
Thus the complex dimension of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}$ is $2\nu$ which
should be contrasted to real manifolds where the corresponding dimension of
the tangent space is $\nu$ (see \cite{Helgason}). The bases vectors $\left\{
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}},\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}\right\}
$ are partial differential operators defined by their action
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}  &  :f\rightarrow\frac{\partial f\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\nonumber\\
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}  &  :f\rightarrow\frac{\partial f\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}%
\end{align}
on functions%
\begin{equation}
f:\mathcal{M}\rightarrow\mathbb{C},
\end{equation}
evaluated at the point $\mathbf{z}$. The action of a general element
\begin{equation}
X=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\left\{  x_{j}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}%
}+x_{j+\nu}\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}\right\}
\end{equation}
on a function $f$ is to produce the directional derivative
\begin{equation}
X\left(  f\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\left\{  x_{j}\frac{\partial f\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}+x_{j+\nu}\frac{\partial f\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\right\}  =\mathbf{x\cdot}Df\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)
\end{equation}
of $f$ in the direction $\mathbf{x,}$ where
\begin{equation}
Df\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\nabla_{\mathbf{\bar{z}}}f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right) \\
\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}
The basis $\left\{  d\bar{z}_{j},dz_{j}\right\}  $ of the cotangent spaces
$\left\{  \mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}^{\ast}\right\}  $ and $\left\{
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}},\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{j}}\right\}
$ of the tangent spaces $\left\{  \mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}\right\}  $ are
biorthogonal i.e.
\begin{align}
dz_{j}\left(  \frac{\partial}{\partial z_{k}}\right)   &  =d\bar{z}_{j}\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{k}}\right)  =\delta_{jk}\nonumber\\
d\bar{z}_{j}\left(  \frac{\partial}{\partial z_{k}}\right)   &  =dz_{j}\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{k}}\right)  =0.
\end{align}
The differential, $df$, (a linear functional on $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}$ and
the best linear approximation to the function $f$ in the neighborhood of the
point $\mathbf{z),}$ can be expanded as
\begin{equation}
df=\sum_{j}\left\{  \frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial
\bar{z}_{j}}d\bar{z}_{j}+\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial
z_{j}}dz_{j}\right\}  .
\end{equation}
One can associate with the functions $f$ with vectors, $X_{f}\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  \in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}}$, that produce derivatives in
the direction of the gradient of $f$ by the definition
\begin{equation}
X_{f}\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  =\sum_{j}\left\{  \frac{\partial f\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}_{j}%
}+\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\frac{\partial
}{\partial z_{j}}\right\}
\end{equation}
and thus extend the action of, $h$, to functions defined on $\mathcal{M}$ by
\begin{equation}
h\left(  X_{f},X_{g}\right)  =2\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  d\bar{z}_{j}\left(  X_{f}\right)  \mathbf{\otimes
}dz_{k}\left(  X_{g}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where again the $\mathbf{z}$ dependence of $h$ is implicit.

If the functions $f$ are holomorphic (i.e. can be expanded in a convergent
power series at each point) then
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}=0\quad1\leq
j\leq\nu.
\end{equation}
In order to distinguish holomorphic from non holomorphic functions one
introduces the notation $f\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  ,$ (even though
$f:\mathcal{M\rightarrow\,}\mathbb{C}$) as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}\neq0\text{
\emph{and }}\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{k}}%
\neq0\text{ for some }j\text{ and }k.
\end{equation}
Rigorously the function $f$ is now considered as the values of $f:\mathcal{M}%
\times\mathcal{M}\rightarrow\mathcal{\,}\mathbb{C}$ restricted to the points
$\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  $ i.e. $\left.  f\left(  \mathbf{z}%
^{\prime}\mathbf{,z}\right)  \right\vert _{\mathbf{z}^{\prime}=\mathbf{z}}$).

The hermitian form $h$ can be decomposed into the sum of a real part,
$\operatorname{Re}h$, which is a real symmetric bilinear form, $\rho$, and $i$
times an imaginary part, $\operatorname{Im}h$, which is an antisymmetric
bilinear form $\omega$, as
\begin{equation}
h=\rho+i\omega,
\end{equation}
which corresponds to decomposing the tensor products $d\bar{z}_{j}%
\mathbf{\otimes}dz_{k}$ into symmetric, "$\mathbf{\vee}$\textbf{"}$\mathbf{,}$
and antisymmetric, "$\mathbf{\wedge}$" tensor products \cite{Greub}%
\begin{equation}
d\bar{z}_{j}\mathbf{\otimes}dz_{k}=d\bar{z}_{j}\mathbf{\vee}dz_{k}+d\bar
{z}_{j}\mathbf{\wedge}dz_{k}.
\end{equation}
(Notational note: In the following we will use $\left[  {}\right]  $
delimiters to signify a secondary dependence that is not the primary focus of
the properties being described). The symmetric form $\rho\left[
\mathbf{z}\right]  $ can be expanded as%
\begin{equation}
\rho\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  =2\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  d\bar{z}_{j}\vee dz_{k}=2\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu
}\left\{  \operatorname{Re}C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  \right\}
d\bar{z}_{j}\mathbf{\otimes}dz_{k}%
\end{equation}
and the antisymmetric form $\omega\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right]  $ as
\begin{equation}
\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  =-2i\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  d\bar{z}_{j}\wedge dz_{k}=-2i\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq
\nu}\left\{  \operatorname{Im}C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)
\right\}  d\bar{z}_{j}\mathbf{\otimes}dz_{k}.
\end{equation}
The real inner product $\rho\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  $ produces a Riemannian
structure on $\mathcal{M}$, while $\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  $ produces
a symplectic structure.

The symplectic form $\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  $ acts on tangent
vectors $\left\{  X_{f},X_{g}\right\}  $ of $\mathcal{M}$ according to
\begin{align}
\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  \left(  X_{f},X_{g}\right)   &
=-\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  \left(  X_{g},X_{f}\right)  =-2i\sum_{1\leq
j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  d\bar{z}_{j}\wedge
dz_{k}\left(  X_{f}\mathbf{\otimes}X_{g}\right) \nonumber\\
&  =-i\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  \left\{
\frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}%
\frac{\partial g\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{k}}-\frac{\partial
f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{k}}\frac{\partial g\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}\right\} \nonumber\\
&  =-i\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\nu}\left\{  \frac{\partial f\left(  \mathbf{z}%
\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}C_{jk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)
\frac{\partial g\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{k}}-\frac{\partial
f\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\overline{C_{jk}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  }\frac{\partial g\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
}{\partial\bar{z}_{k}}\right\}  ,
\end{align}
which can be expressed in matrix notation as
\begin{equation}
\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  \left(  X_{f},X_{g}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial f}{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right) \\
-i\overline{\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  } & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\left(  \frac{\partial f}{\partial\mathbf{z}}\right)  _{j}=\frac{\partial
f}{\partial z_{j}}.
\end{equation}
The symplectic form $\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]  $ defines a generalized
Poisson Bracket $\left\{  ,\right\}  _{\mathbf{z}}$, (which is $\mathbf{z}$
dependent), by
\begin{equation}
\left\{  f,g\right\}  _{\mathbf{z}}=\omega\left[  \mathbf{z}\right]
^{-1}\left(  X_{f},X_{g}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial f}{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\overline{\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  }^{-1}\\
-i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  ^{-1} & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \label{PB}%
\end{equation}
(Notational Note: Unless it is essential the $\mathbf{z}$ dependence of the
Poisson Bracket will be implicit).

The symplectic form and the Poisson Bracket create a generalized phase space
structure on $\mathcal{M},$ where real valued functions, $f_{\mathcal{O}}$,
defined on $\mathcal{M}$ by the expectation values of self adjoint quantum
observables $\mathcal{O}$ as
\begin{equation}
f_{\mathcal{O}}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z},z}\right)  =\frac{\left\langle \left.
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert O\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle },
\end{equation}
can be considered as "classical" observables.

\subsection{Coherent State Tangent Spaces}

Hilbert spaces are self dual i.e. they are isomorphic to the space of bounded
linear functions defined on them, which is depicted in Dirac notation as
\begin{equation}
J:\left\vert v\right\rangle \rightarrow\left\langle v\right\vert .
\end{equation}
The map $\Phi$, from the complex space $\mathbb{C}^{\nu}$ to the CS manifold
$\mathcal{M}$, is the inverse of a coordinate chart, $\Phi^{-1}$, of
$\mathcal{M}$ (see Appendix \ref{manifold})
\begin{equation}
\Phi^{-1}:\mathcal{M}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{\nu},
\end{equation}
which is given by
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z}=\Phi^{-1}\left(  \exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}z_{j}%
Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle \right)  \Leftrightarrow
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  =\exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}z_{j}%
Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle . \label{chart1}%
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  Y_{j}\right\}  $ are the generators of the coset $G/K$ in
$\mathcal{B}\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)  ,$ as described in section
\ref{cohstate}. We can extend this chart to the complex conjugate space
$\overline{\mathbb{C}^{\nu}}$ by using the isomorphism $J,$ after noting that
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{\bar{z}}=\overline{\Phi^{-1}\left(  \exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu
}z_{j}Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle \right)  }%
\equiv\overline{\Phi^{-1}}\left(  \exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}z_{j}%
Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle \right)  ,
\end{equation}
which gives
\begin{equation}
\bar{\Phi}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}\right)  =\exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu
}z_{j}Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
and finally
\begin{equation}
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}\right)  =J\left(  \exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq
j\leq\nu}z_{j}Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi_{ref}\right\rangle \right)
=\left\langle \Phi_{ref}\right\vert \exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\bar
{z}_{j}Y_{j}^{\dagger}\right\}  .
\end{equation}
The derivatives $\left\{  \left.  \frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}%
_{0}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\}  ,\left\{
\left.  \frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\bar{z}_{j}}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\}  $ are bases for the tangent
subspaces $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  1,0\right)  }$ and
$\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }$ respectively, (see
Appendix \ref{manifold}). These basis vectors are easily obtained from eq.
$\left(  \ref{chart1}\right)  $ as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  }{\partial z_{k}}=Y_{k}%
\exp\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}z_{0j}Y_{j}\right\}  \left\vert \Phi
_{ref}\right\rangle =Y_{k}\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle
\end{equation}
and eq. $\left(  \ref{chart2}\right)  $ as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{k}%
}=\left\langle \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  \right\vert Y_{k}^{\dag}.
\end{equation}
The full tangent space $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}$ is given by the direct
sum of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  1,0\right)  }$ and
$\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}=\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  1,0\right)
}\oplus\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }%
\end{equation}
and the basis $\left\{  \left.  \left\{  \frac{\partial\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}}\right\}  ,\left\{
\frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}\right\}
\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\}  $ is adapted to this decomposition. The
spaces $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  1,0\right)  }$ and
$\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }$ can be considered as the
images of the space $\mathbb{C}^{\nu}$ under the linear maps $d\Phi\left[
\mathbf{z}_{0}\right]  $ and $d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right]  ,$
which are the differentials of the coordinate chart inverse $\Phi$ at the
points $\mathbf{z}_{0}$ and $\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}$, i.e.
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right]   &  :\mathbb{C}^{\nu}\rightarrow
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  1,0\right)  }\\
d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right]  \left(  e_{k}\right)   &  =\frac
{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  }{\partial z_{j}}%
\end{align}
and
\end{subequations}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right]   &  :\mathbb{C}^{\nu}\rightarrow
\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}^{\left(  0,1\right)  }\\
d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right]  \left(  e_{k}\right)   &
=\frac{\partial\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\bar{z}_{j}%
},
\end{align}
where $\left\{  \left.  e_{k}\right\vert 1\leq k\leq\nu\right\}  $ is a basis
for $\mathbb{C}^{\nu}$. For convenience we will denote the inverse linear
coordinate map into $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}$ as $d\Phi\left[
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right]  $, where
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right]  :\mathbb{C}^{\nu
}\oplus\mathbb{C}^{\nu}\rightarrow d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0}\right]
\left(  \mathbb{C}^{\nu}\right)  \oplus d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{z}_{0}\right]
\left(  \mathbb{C}^{\nu}\right)  \equiv\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}.
\end{equation}


\section{Quantum Dynamics on the Coherent State Manifold\label{quantdyn}}

One is especially interested in the evolution of quantum states induced by a
quantum Hamiltonian $H\left(  t\right)  $ restricted to the manifold
$\mathcal{M}$ producing "classical" paths \textrm{z:}$\mathbb{R\rightarrow
}\mathcal{M}$\textrm{ }that\textrm{ }correspond to quantum paths $\left\vert
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle $. These paths
are partially generated, via the principle of stationary action (as discussed
in Appendix \ref{pathinteg}), by "classical" Hamiltonians $\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z},t\right)  $ defined as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z},t\right)
=\frac{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)
\right\vert H\left(  t\right)  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)
\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)
\right)  \right\rangle },
\end{equation}
and are solutions to generalized classical equations of motion
\begin{align}
-i\frac{d\mathrm{z}_{j}}{dt}  &  =\left\{  \mathrm{z}_{j},\mathcal{E}\right\}
\nonumber\\
i\frac{d\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{j}}{dt}  &  =\left\{  \mathrm{\bar{z}}%
_{j},\mathcal{E}\right\}  \label{EOM}%
\end{align}
for the Hamiltonian function $\mathcal{E}$. The quantum paths are only
partially determined by eq. $\left(  \ref{EOM}\right)  $ as one must also
solve the independent equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\sigma}{dt}=-\operatorname{Im}\left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}%
\mathrm{z}_{j}\frac{\partial\Lambda}{\partial\mathrm{z}_{j}}\right\}
-\mathcal{E} \label{phase}%
\end{equation}
to determine a time dependent phase, $\sigma$, to obtain the quantum path
\cite{K&S}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{\eta}_{j}\left(  t\right)  =e^{\sigma\left(  t\right)  }\mathrm{z}%
_{j}\left(  t\right)  .
\end{equation}
If $\mathcal{M}$ is the manifold of $U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)
/U\left(  1\right)  $-CS's then these equations determine the same paths as
the TDSE otherwise they provide approximations to these paths.

To facilitate numerical calculations it is convenient to consider the
equations eq. $\left(  \ref{EOM}\right)  $ in the matrix form
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{d\mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}\\
\frac{d\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\overline{\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }^{-1}\\
-i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  ^{-1} & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}%
,t\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}%
,t\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  . \label{eom}%
\end{equation}
The metric matrix, $\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  $, and the gradient, $D^{1}%
\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z},t\right)  $, of the
energy function $\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}%
,t\right)  $ are given in Appendix \ref{Matrices}. One should note that the
phase space, $\mathcal{M}$, is not in general flat and hence the Poisson
Brackets are position dependent, a situation akin to problems in General
Relativity. However the equations eq. $\left(  \ref{eom}\right)  $ can in
principle be integrated and solutions found by standard differential equation
methods \cite{DE}. In appendix \ref{solution} we describe a method based on
local quadratic approximations that is related to the linear response of the system.

Stationary paths $\mathrm{z}_{0}\left(  t\right)  $ have constant values
$\mathbf{z}_{c}$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{z}_{0}\left(  t\right)  =\mathbf{z}_{c}\quad\forall t.
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{z}_{c}$ is a critical point of $\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}_{0},t\right)  .$ Eq. $\left(
\ref{eom}\right)  $ implies that $D^{1}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}%
_{0}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}_{0},t\right)  =0$ $\forall t$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }\left\{  \frac{\partial
\mathcal{H}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0}\mathbf{,}\mathrm{z}_{0},t\right)
}{\partial\mathbf{x}}-\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0}\mathbf{,}%
\mathrm{z}_{0},t\right)  \frac{\partial\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}%
_{0}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }%
{\partial\mathbf{x}}\right\}  =0
\end{equation}
which thus gives the equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c},t\right)
}{\partial\mathbf{x}}\equiv\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{x}}=\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  \frac{\partial\mathcal{S}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{x}} \label{TIeq}%
\end{equation}
for $\mathbf{z}_{c}.$If the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is the manifold of
$U\left(  \mathcal{H}^{N}\right)  /U\left(  1\right)  $-CS's then this
equation \emph{is} the Time Independent Schr\"{o}dinger Equation (TISE), while
if the manifold is contained in the manifold of $U\left(  \mathcal{H}%
^{N}\right)  /U\left(  1\right)  $-CS's eq. $\left(  \ref{TIeq}\right)  $
produces non linear equations that determine approximate solutions to the TISE.

The Hessian $D^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}%
_{c}\right)  $ of the energy at critical points is made up of blocks of the
form
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}%
_{c}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{x}\partial\mathbf{y}}=\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}%
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  }\left\{  \frac
{\partial^{2}\mathcal{H}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)
}{\partial\mathbf{x}\partial\mathbf{y}}-\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  \frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{S}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{x}%
\partial\mathbf{y}}\right\}
\end{equation}
for $\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y=z},\mathbf{\bar{z}}$ and gives information of the
about the nature of critical point $\mathbf{z}_{c}$ i.e. whether it is a
maximum, minimum or saddle point of the energy function $\mathcal{E}.$ It also
gives information about the linear response of the system to external time
dependent potentials, as we discuss section \ref{linres}.

\subsection{Linearization of the EOM}

In the case of a curved phase space there are two stages to linearization. The
first stage is choose a specific point in the phase space and work locally in
the flat phase space formed by the tangent space about that point, the second
is to make a quadratic approximation to the effect of classical Hamiltonian in
this flat space. Most often the point chosen on the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is
an equilibrium or critical point of the Hamiltonian \cite{Arnold}. The
classical Hamiltonian, $\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)
$, is a \emph{real} analytic function and can be expanded as a power series
about an arbitrary point $\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  $ in the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  =\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +D^{1}%
\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}\right)
+\frac{1}{2}D^{2}\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +\cdots, \label{enexp}%
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}$ and $\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}$
are vectors in the tangent space $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$. If
the Hamiltonian is globally analytic this power series converges for all
complex coordinates $\mathbf{z,}$ otherwise it will converge in some
neighborhood of $\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}.$ This form of $\mathcal{E}$ can be
used in the EOM\ eq. $\left(  \ref{eom}\right)  $, to determine solution paths
\textrm{z}$\left(  t\right)  $ that belong to the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ in
terms of coordinate vectors that belong to $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}}$, through the inverse coordinate chart $\Phi$ (see Appendix
\ref{functions}). The same expansion determines, in general different, paths
\textrm{z}$_{F}\left(  t\right)  $ in the flat phase space $\mathcal{T}%
_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$ when a fixed Poisson Bracket $\left\{  {}\right\}
_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$, corresponding to a fixed metric of $\mathcal{T}%
_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$, is used in eq. $\left(  \ref{eom}\right)  .$

The first order term eq. $\left(  \ref{enexp}\right)  $ is given by
\begin{equation}
D^{1}\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{z-\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac
{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\\
\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and the second order term
\begin{equation}
D^{2}\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z-z}_{0}\right)
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0} & \mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}%
}\partial\mathbf{z}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}
& \frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{z}-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\\
\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}
The quadratic approximation, $\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $, to $\mathcal{E}%
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $ around the point $\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
},\mathbf{z}\right)  =\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +D^{1}\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
-\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{z-\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +\frac{1}{2}%
D^{2}\mathcal{E}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}-\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{equation}
and the EOM eq. $\left(  \ref{z0evol}\right)  $ can be linearized to give to
give tangent space paths that satisfy
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{\dot{z}}_{L}\left(  t\right)  =-i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  ^{-1}\frac{\partial
\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}%
}_{L}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }%
{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}, \label{linEOM}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(
\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{L}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{z}}+\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\cdot\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{L}\left(  t\right)
-\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\right)  +\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}\partial\mathbf{z}}\cdot\left(  \mathrm{z}_{L}\left(
t\right)  -\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  .
\end{equation}
The solution to eq. $\left(  \ref{linEOM}\right)  $ is given in Appendix
\label{LinEOM} and is non holomorphic unless $\frac{\partial^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}=\mathbf{0}$.

If $\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  $ is a
critical point of $\mathcal{E}_{0}$ the linearized classical Hamiltonian
$\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  $ becomes
\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
},\mathbf{z}\right)   &  =\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \nonumber\\
&  +\frac{1}{2}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{\bar{z}}-\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0} & \mathbf{z}-\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{z}}-\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\\
\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \label{e2}%
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
D_{\mathbf{xy}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left\{
D_{\mathbf{xy}}^{2}\mathcal{H}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  -\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  D_{\mathbf{xy}}^{2}%
\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\}  .
\end{equation}
This leads to the linearized EOM for paths $\mathrm{\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)
$ in the tangent space $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$
\begin{align}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{d\mathrm{\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}\\
\frac{d\mathrm{\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}%
\end{array}
\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
-i\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(
\mathrm{\bar{\eta}}_{L}\left(  t\right)  \mathrm{,\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{2}\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(
\mathrm{\bar{\eta}}_{L}\left(  t\right)  \mathrm{,\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right) \nonumber\\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
-i\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}%
_{c},\mathbf{z}_{c}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathrm{\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right) \\
\mathrm{\bar{\eta}}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{\eta}_{L}\left(  t\right)  =\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)
-\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{equation}
and the approximate solution paths $\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{L}\left(
t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  $ with initial value
$\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  $ given
by
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{L}\left(  t\right) \\
\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\exp\left\{  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
-i\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  t\right\}  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\\
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \label{linpath}%
\end{align}


The solution path $\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)  \in\mathcal{T}%
_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$ to this equation is described in Appendix
\ref{solution} and corresponds to the following path of states in the CS
tangent space
\begin{equation}
\left\vert d\Phi\left[  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(  t\right)
\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq k\leq\nu}\left\{  Y_{k}\left\vert \Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle \mathrm{z}_{Lk}\left(  t\right)
+\left\langle \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
Y_{k}^{\dag}\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{Lk}\left(  t\right)  \right\}  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{Y}\left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
& \left\langle \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dag}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right) \\
\mathrm{\bar{z}}_{L}\left(  t\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}


\section{Linear Response\label{linres}}

If the external radiation fields are of low intensity and the molecular system
is initially in a stationary state, it is sufficient to consider the linear
response of the molecular state to the applied fields, which can be expressed
in terms of a set of linear response functions. In this section we first
describe the structure of these functions in the standard quantum mechanical
fashion, then in terms of the CS\ formalism.

\subsection{Hilbert Space Setting\label{Hilset}}

\subsubsection{Exact Linear Response\label{exlinres}}

Time dependent perturbation theory gives that the first order change,
$\left\vert \delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle $, of the
stationary state path $\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)
\right\rangle $ of the unperturbed Hamiltonian $H_{0}$ due to an external
potential $F\left(  t\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle
=e^{-iH_{0}t}\left\{  -i\int_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)
dt^{\prime}\right\}  \left\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle , \label{deltapsi}%
\end{equation}
where%
\begin{equation}
F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  =e^{iH_{o}t^{\prime}}F\left(  t^{\prime
}\right)  e^{-iH_{o}t^{\prime}},
\end{equation}
the stationary path
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle =e^{-iH_{0}%
t}\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle =e^{-iE_{0}%
t}\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
and $\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\right\rangle $ is a normalized solution to
the TISE for the unperturbed Hamiltonian $H_{0}$ corresponding to the energy
$E_{0}$. It is important to note that during such changes the norm of
$\left\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  +\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(
t\right)  \right\rangle $ remains constant to first order as
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  +\delta^{1}%
\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)
+\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle \simeq\left\langle
\left.  \Psi_{0}\right\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle +\left\langle \left.
\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(
t\right)  \right\rangle +\left\langle \left.  \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(
t\right)  \right\vert \delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}
&  \left\langle \left.  \delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert
\mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle +\left\langle \left.
\mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert \delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(
t\right)  \right\rangle =\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\left\langle \left.  -i\int_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime
}\right)  dt^{\prime}\Psi_{0}\right\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle +\left\langle
\Psi_{0}\left\vert -i\int_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime
}\right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle =\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad i\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert \int
_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}\right.  \Psi
_{0}\right\rangle -i\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert \int_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(
t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}\right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle =0.
\end{align}
Using the expression eq. $\left(  \ref{deltapsi}\right)  $ for $\left\vert
\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle $ it is straightforward
to show that the first order change, $\delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle
_{0}\left(  t\right)  $, of the expectation value of an observable $A$, from
its stationary state value $\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}$ after a time $t$
is
\begin{align}
&  \delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}\left(  t\right)  =\left\{
\left\langle \left.  \mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert
A\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\rangle +\left\langle \left.
\delta^{1}\mathrm{\Psi}\left(  t\right)  \right\vert A\mathrm{\Psi}_{0}\left(
t\right)  \right\rangle \right\} \nonumber\\
&  \left.  =\right.  -i\left\{  \left\langle e^{-iH_{0}t}\Psi_{0}\left\vert
Ae^{-iH_{0}t}\int_{0}^{t}F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}\Psi
_{0}\right.  \right\rangle -\left\langle \left.  e^{-iH_{0}t}\int_{0}^{t}%
F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}\Psi_{0}\right\vert Ae^{-iH_{0}%
t}\Psi_{0}\right\rangle \right\} \nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\left.
=\right.  -i\int_{0}^{t}\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert \left[  A_{H}\left(
t\right)  ,F_{H}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  \right]  \right.  \Psi
_{0}\right\rangle dt^{\prime}, \label{tdpt1}%
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
A_{H}\left(  t\right)  =e^{iH_{o}t}Ae^{-iH_{o}t}.
\end{equation}
Equation eq. $\left(  \ref{tdpt1}\right)  $ can be rearranged to give
\begin{equation}
\delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}\left(  t\right)  =-i\int_{0}%
^{t}\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert \left[  A_{H}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)
,F\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  \right]  \right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle
dt^{\prime} \label{resquant}%
\end{equation}
and finally
\begin{equation}
\delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}\left(  t\right)  =-i\int_{-\infty
}^{\infty}\theta_{+}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  \left\langle \Psi
_{0}\left\vert \left[  A_{H}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  ,F\left(  t^{\prime
}\right)  \right]  \right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle dt^{\prime},
\end{equation}
where $F\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  =0$ for $t^{\prime}<0.$ The response of
expectation values of a system initially in a stationary state $\left\vert
\Psi_{0}\right\rangle $ to an external time dependent perturbing potential,
$F(t)$,
\begin{equation}
F(t)=\sum_{k,l}f_{kl}\left(  t\right)  a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}%
\end{equation}
is then given by
\begin{equation}
\delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}\left(  t\right)  =\int_{-\infty
}^{\infty}\sum_{k,l}\left\{  -i\theta_{+}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)
\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert \left[  A_{H}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)
,a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\right]  \right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle \right\}
f_{kl}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}. \label{TDPT2}%
\end{equation}
Eq. $\left(  \ref{TDPT2}\right)  $ can be expressed succinctly by defining
Response Functions $\left\{  R_{Akl}\left(  \tau\right)  \right\}  $ by
\begin{equation}
R_{Akl}\left(  \tau\right)  =-i\theta_{+}\left(  \tau\right)  \left\langle
\Psi_{0}\left\vert \left[  A_{H}\left(  \tau\right)  ,a_{k}^{\dagger}%
a_{l}\right]  \right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle , \label{resdef}%
\end{equation}
to give
\begin{equation}
\delta^{1}\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}\left(  t\right)  =\sum_{k,l}%
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}R_{Akl}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  f_{kl}\left(
t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}. \label{res}%
\end{equation}


The response of any one particle property of a system can be formulated in
terms of the response, $\delta\mathbf{\rho}\left(  t\right)  $, of the First
Order Reduced Density Matrix (FORDM) $\mathbf{\rho}$ from its equilibrium
value $\mathbf{\rho}\left(  0\right)  $, which has matrix elements
\begin{equation}
\rho\left(  0\right)  _{ji}=\left\langle \left.  \Psi_{0}\right\vert
a_{i}^{\dagger}a_{j}\Psi_{0}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
In the case of a weak external field one can neglect terms higher than first
order in $F$ and use eq. $\left(  \ref{res}\right)  $ to obtain the linear
response of $\mathbf{\rho}\left(  0\right)  $ as
\begin{equation}
\delta^{1}\mathbf{\rho}\left(  t\right)  =\sum_{k,l}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty
}R_{\mathbf{\rho}kl}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  f_{kl}\left(  t^{\prime
}\right)  dt^{\prime}.
\end{equation}
The linear responses $\left\{  \delta\rho_{ij}\left(  t\right)  \right\}  $ of
the matrix elements of $\mathbf{\rho}\left(  0\right)  $ can be cast in the
form
\begin{equation}
\delta\rho_{ij}\left(  t\right)  =\sum_{k,l}\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}%
R_{ijkl}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  f_{kl}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)
dt^{\prime},
\end{equation}
where the one particle linear response functions $\left\{  R_{ijkl}\left(
t\right)  \right\}  $ are defined as
\begin{equation}
R_{ijkl}\left(  t\right)  =-i\theta_{+}\left(  t\right)  \left\langle \Psi
_{0}\left\vert \left[  a_{Hj}^{\dagger}\left(  t\right)  a_{Hi}\left(
t\right)  ,a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\right]  \right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
which can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
R_{ijkl}\left(  t\right)  =-i\theta_{+}\left(  t\right)  \left\{  \left\langle
\Psi_{0}\left\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{i}e^{-i\left(  H_{0}-E_{0}\right)
t}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle -\left\langle \Psi
_{0}\left\vert a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}e^{i\left(  H_{0}-E_{0}\right)  t}%
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{i}\right.  \Psi_{0}\right\rangle \right\}  .
\end{equation}
Taking the Laplace transform \cite{Dunford} of $R_{lkij}\left(  t\right)  $ we
obtain
\begin{equation}
R_{ijkl}\left(  \zeta\right)  =\sum_{n\neq0}\left\{  \frac{\left\langle
\Psi_{0}\left\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{i}\Psi_{n}\right.  \right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\Psi_{0}\right.
\right\rangle }{\zeta-\left(  E_{n}-E_{0}\right)  }-\frac{\left\langle
\Psi_{0}\left\vert a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\Psi_{n}\right.  \right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{i}\Psi_{0}\right.
\right\rangle }{\zeta+\left(  E_{n}-E_{0}\right)  }\right\}  .
\end{equation}
The Laplace transformed response functions $\left\{  R_{ijkl}\left(
\zeta\right)  \right\}  $ form a hermitian matrix for real $\zeta$, that can
be diagonalized to give
\begin{equation}
\tilde{R}_{KL}\left(  \zeta\right)  =g_{K}\left(  \zeta\right)  \delta
_{KL}=\sum_{n\neq0}\left\{  \frac{\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert T_{K}%
\Psi_{n}\right.  \right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert T_{K}^{\dagger
}\Psi_{0}\right.  \right\rangle }{\zeta-\left(  E_{n}-E_{0}\right)  }%
-\frac{\left\langle \Psi_{0}\left\vert T_{K}\Psi_{n}\right.  \right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert T_{K}^{\dagger}\Psi_{0}\right.  \right\rangle
}{\zeta+\left(  E_{n}-E_{0}\right)  }\right\}  ,
\end{equation}
where the normal mode excitation operators are
\begin{equation}
T_{K}^{\dagger}=\sum_{i,j}T_{Kij}a_{i}^{\dagger}a_{j}.
\end{equation}
The poles of the Laplace transform occur at $\pm\left(  E_{n}-E_{0}\right)  $
and the residues $\left\vert \left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert T_{K}^{\dagger
}\Psi_{0}\right.  \right\rangle \right\vert ^{2}$ are transition probabilities
that the operator $T_{K}^{\dagger}$ will cause a transition from the
stationary state $\left\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle $ to the stationary state
$\left\vert \Psi_{n}\right\rangle $. In general the excited states can be
expressed in terms of the operators $\left\{  T_{K}^{\dagger}\right\}  $ as
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{n}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq m\leq N}\sum_{K_{1}\cdots K_{m}%
}c_{K_{1}\cdots K_{m}}^{n}T_{K_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots T_{K_{m}}^{\dagger
}\left\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle . \label{state_exp}%
\end{equation}


If the radiation field is weak, which justifies the use of linear response,
one is focusing on single photon processes. If such radiation
\emph{overwhelmingly} produces excited states $\left\{  \left\vert \Psi
_{K}\right\rangle \right\}  $ then one might assume that the transition
probabilities $\left\{  \left\vert \left\langle \Psi_{n}\left\vert
T_{K}^{\dagger}\Psi_{0}\right.  \right\rangle \right\vert ^{2}\right\}  $ are
close to $100\%$ and hence
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{K}\right\rangle \approx T_{K}^{\dagger}\left\vert \Psi
_{0}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
and $\left\{  T_{K}^{\dagger}\right\}  $ are \emph{state} excitation
operators. However, for observable processes to be initiated by single photons
this transition probability does not have to be $100\%$ , it just needs to be
reasonably large, so that interactions between photons and molecules are
likely to occur. Thus it is not necessary for exact $T_{K}^{\dagger}$, nor
approximations to it, to be state excitation operators.

\subsubsection{Approximate Linear Response\label{ap_linres}}

Approximations to linear response can be formulated in terms of approximations to

\begin{description}
\item[$\cdot$] $\left\vert \Psi_{0}\right\rangle $ i.e. restricting the nature
of the reference state by any standard approximation ansatz, e.g. Multi
Configurational Self Consistent Field (MCSCF), CI, Coupled Cluster, etc...,

\item[$\cdot$] $H_{0}$ e.g. express $H_{0}=h+V$ where $h$ is an independent
particle Hamiltonian and $V$ is a correlation potential and expand in terms of
powers of $V,$

\item[$\cdot$] $\left\vert \Psi_{n}\right\rangle $ by truncating the expansion
eq. $\left(  \ref{state_exp}\right)  $ at various orders, which is the
approach pioneered by Osvaldo Goscinski via the introduction of super
operators and operator manifolds \cite{Osvaldo}.
\end{description}

\subsection{Linear Response in a Coherent State Setting\label{CSset}}

In this section we show that \emph{classical }linear response theory applied
to the EOM eq. $\left(  \ref{eom}\right)  $ in the CS\ manifold $\mathcal{M}$
is \emph{equivalent }to quantum linear response theory. This allows one to
design, extend and see the implications of approximations more clearly.

\subsubsection{Full Equations of Motion for any Manifold $\mathcal{M}$}

For a manifold of CS's $\left\{  \left\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle \right\}  $ the classical Hamiltonian, $\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)  $, corresponding to the Hamiltonian
containing the time dependent perturbation is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)  =\frac{\left\langle
\left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \left(  H_{0}+F\left(
t\right)  \right)  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle
\left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}%
\right)  \right\rangle }=\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}%
,\mathbf{z}\right)  +\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)
\end{equation}
where the classical observables $\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
},\mathbf{z}\right)  $ and $\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}%
t\right)  $ are given by
\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)   &  =\frac
{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert H_{0}%
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle }\nonumber\\
\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)   &  =\frac
{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert F\left(
t\right)  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle }.
\end{align}
It should be observed that the external potential function, $\mathcal{F}%
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)  ,$ is linear in the FORDM
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)  =\sum_{1\leq
k,l\leq\nu}f_{kl}\left(  t\right)  \frac{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}%
\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle }=\sum_{1\leq
k,l\leq\nu}f_{kl}\left(  t\right)  \rho_{lk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}%
},\mathbf{z}\right)  , \label{expot}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\rho_{lk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  =\frac{\left\langle
\left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{l}%
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathbf{z}\right)
\right\rangle }.
\end{equation}
The restriction of the quantum evolution to the CS manifold is then given by
the solution paths $\left\{  \mathrm{\bar{z},z}\right\}  $ to the equations
eq. $\left(  \ref{EOM}\right)  ,$ that can expressed in matrix form as
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{d\mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}\\
\frac{d\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right) \\
-i\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z},z}\mathbf{,}t\right)
}{\partial\mathrm{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z},z}\mathbf{,}t\right)
}{\partial\mathrm{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  , \label{MEOM}%
\end{equation}
where the metric matrix along the paths, $\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}%
}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  $, is given by
\begin{align}
\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)   &  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}%
}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right)  }\left\{
\frac{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)
\right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }\right. \nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\left.  -\,\mathcal{S}\left(  \left(
\mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)
\right)  \frac{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)
\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }\frac{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(
\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }{\left\langle \left.
\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \Phi\left(
\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle }\right\}
\end{align}
and the gradient of the classical Hamiltonian by
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z},z}\mathbf{,}t\right)
}{\partial\mathrm{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z},z}\mathbf{,}t\right)
}{\partial\mathrm{z}}%
\end{array}
\right) \nonumber\\
&  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right)  }\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\left\{  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)
\right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}H\left(  t\right)  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}%
\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle -\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar
{z},z}\mathbf{,}t\right)  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}%
\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle \right\} \\
\left\{  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(  t\right)  \right)
\right\vert H\left(  t\right)  \mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle -\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}%
,z}\mathbf{,}t\right)  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  \right\rangle \right\}
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}
The evolution paths, $\rho_{lk}\left(  t\right)  ,$ of the matrix elements of
the FORDM $\mathbf{\rho}\left(  t\right)  $ can be expressed in terms of the
solutions of eq. $\left(  \ref{MEOM}\right)  $ as $\rho_{lk}\left(  t\right)
=\rho_{lk}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}\left(
t\right)  \right)  $ or as solutions to the EOM
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\rho_{lk}\left(  t\right)  }{dt}=\left\{  \rho_{lk}\left(  t\right)
,\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}},\mathrm{z},t\right)  \right\}
\end{equation}
for the paths determined by eq. $\left(  \ref{MEOM}\right)  .$ It should be
observed that \emph{no matter} the form of the classical Hamiltonian
$\mathcal{E},$ solutions to equation eq. $\left(  \ref{MEOM}\right)  $
describe paths in the CS manifold $\mathcal{M}$ which correspond to paths of
states in $\mathcal{H}^{N}$, consequently any approximations to $\mathcal{E}%
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t\right)  $ or $\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $ still lead to states in
$\mathcal{M\subset H}^{N}.$

\subsubsection{Full Classical Linear Response}

As the CS manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is a classical phase space it is natural to
directly utilize classical linear response theory, which is based on classical
first order time dependent perturbation theory \cite{Thirring1}. However as
this phase space is curved one has a choice of applying the perturbation
theory about a point $\mathbf{z}_{0}\in\mathcal{M}$ in the manifold
$\mathcal{M}$ including perturbations to the metric, or in the tangent space
$\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}$. It is easier and more directly comparable to
the Quantum Theory to perform the latter. This produces first order changes of
the flat phase space paths given by
\begin{equation}
\delta\mathrm{z}_{L}\left(  t\right)  =\int_{0}^{t}\left\{  \mathrm{z}%
_{0L}\left(  t\right)  ,\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0L}\left(
t^{\prime}\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  \mathbf{,}%
t^{\prime}\right)  \right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}}dt^{\prime},
\end{equation}
where the unperturbed evolution path with initial value $\mathbf{z}_{0}$ is
given as
\begin{align}
&  \mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t\right)  =e^{\mathcal{L}_{0}t}\mathbf{z}%
_{0}=\mathbf{z}_{0}+\nonumber\\
&  \sum_{n\geq1}\int_{0}^{t}\int_{t_{1}}^{t}\cdots\int_{t_{n-1}}^{t}\left\{
\left\{  \cdots\left\{  \mathbf{z}_{0}\mathbf{,}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  \right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}}%
,\cdots,\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right)
\right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}},\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}%
_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  \right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}}dt_{1}dt_{2}\cdots
dt_{n}. \label{clasol}%
\end{align}
In matrix form the equation for the unperturbed paths, $\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(
t\right)  $, leading to the solution eq. $\left(  \ref{classol}\right)  $, is
\begin{align}
\mathrm{\dot{z}}_{0L}\left(  t\right)   &  =\left\{  \mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(
t\right)  \mathbf{,}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0L}\left(
t\right)  ,\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  \right\}  _{\mathbf{z}%
_{0}}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0}\\
-i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  ^{-1} &
\mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0L}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0L}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right) \nonumber\\
&  \left.  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\right.
=-i\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right)  ^{-1}%
\frac{\partial\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathrm{\bar{z}}_{0L}\left(  t\right)
,\mathrm{z}_{0L}\left(  t\right)  \right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}.
\label{z0evol}%
\end{align}
The solution of equation eq. $\left(  \ref{z0evol}\right)  $ is discussed in
Appendix \ref{solution}.

The linear response of the classical observables $\left\{  \rho_{lk}\right\}
$ about an initial point $\rho_{lk}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}%
_{0}\right)  $ is given by
\begin{equation}
\delta\rho_{lk}\left(  t\right)  =-\int_{0}^{t}\left\{  \rho_{Hlk}\left(
t\right)  ,\mathcal{F}_{H}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t^{\prime
}\right)  \right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}}dt^{\prime} \label{dmres}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
\rho_{Hji}\left(  t\right)   &  =e^{\mathcal{L}_{0}t}\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0}\right) \nonumber\\
\mathcal{F}_{H}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z,}t^{\prime}\right)   &
=e^{\mathcal{L}_{0}t^{\prime}}\mathcal{F}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}}%
_{0},\mathbf{z}_{0},t^{\prime}\right)  ,
\end{align}
which can be expanded in the same fashion as eq. $\left(  \ref{clasol}\right)
.$ Using the expansion eq. $\left(  \ref{expot}\right)  $ the response of the
density matrix elements eq. $\left(  \ref{dmres}\right)  $ becomes
\begin{equation}
\delta\rho_{ji}\left(  t\right)  =\sum_{r,s}\int_{0}^{t}\left\{  \rho
_{Hji}\left(  t\right)  ,\rho_{Hsr}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  \right\}
_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}f_{rs}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}=\sum_{r,s}%
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}R_{ijrs}\left(  t,t^{\prime}\right)  f_{rs}\left(
t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime},
\end{equation}
where we have introduced response functions
\begin{equation}
R_{ijrs}\left(  t,t^{\prime}\right)  =\theta_{+}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)
\left\{  \rho_{jiH}\left(  t\right)  ,\rho_{sr}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)
\right\}  _{\mathbf{z}_{0}}. \label{classres}%
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Truncated Classical Linear Response}

Basing the effects of the perturbation on the time evolution in the tangent
space on the full unperturbed Hamiltonian $\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $, is complicated and does not produce
RPA\ equations that correspond to ones obtained from standard quantum theory.
However using the quadratic approximation $\mathcal{E}_{02}\left[
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  $ to
$\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $ and the linear
approximations $\left\{  \rho_{1ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \right\}  $ to the observables $\left\{
\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  \right\}  $ at a critical
point $\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  $
of $\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{z}},\mathbf{z}\right)  $ does
reproduce the RPA\ and generalized RPA. The approximations are based on the
expansion (see Appendix \ref{functions})%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq n\leq\infty}%
D^{n}\mathcal{O}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  .
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{E}_{02}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  $ given by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{02}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  =\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +D^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
&  \frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}\partial\bar{z}}%
}=\nonumber\\
&  \frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }\left\{  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}H_{0}%
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle -\,\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
\right\} \nonumber\\
&  \frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}\partial z}%
}=\nonumber\\
&  \frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }\left\{  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}H_{0}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle -\,\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \left\langle
\left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}%
^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle \right\}
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z\partial\bar{z}}}  &
=\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}\partial z}%
}^{\dagger}=\overline{\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar
{z}\partial z}}}\nonumber\\
\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z\partial z}}  &
=\overline{\frac{\partial^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}\partial\bar{z}%
}}}.
\end{align}


The linear approximations $\left\{  \rho_{1ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \right\}  $ to the FORDM\ matrix elements
are given by
\begin{equation}
\rho_{1ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]
\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  =\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left(
\mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right)  , \label{linapprox}%
\end{equation}
where the first order derivatives of $\rho_{ji}$ are
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}  &  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left\{
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}a_{i}^{\dagger}a_{j}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  \right\rangle -\,\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle \right\} \nonumber\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}  &  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left\{
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
a_{i}^{\dagger}a_{j}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle -\,\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle \right\}  .
\end{align}
The time evolution produced by $\mathcal{E}_{02}\left[  \mathbf{\bar
{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  $ on the flat phase space
$\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}}$ produces coordinate paths
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{\eta}}\left(  t\right) \\
\mathbf{\eta}\left(  t\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =e^{\mathcal{L}_{02}t}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \nonumber\\
&  =\exp\left\{  i\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
-\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  t\right\}  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\\
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  . \label{linevol}%
\end{align}
This leads to
\begin{equation}
\rho_{1jiH}\left(  \tau\right)  =\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  +\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  e^{i\mathbb{M}t}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}\\
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{M}$ is defined by eq. $\left(  \ref{linevol}\right)  $. This
can be equivalently written as
\begin{align}
\rho_{1jiH}\left(  \tau\right)   &  =\rho_{1ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma
}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  \left\{  \left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta}%
}\left(  \tau\right)  \mathbf{,\eta}\left(  \tau\right)  \right)  \right\}
=\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
+D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  e^{i\mathbb{M}t}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\eta}}\left(  0\right)
\mathbf{,\eta}\left(  0\right)  \right) \nonumber\\
&  =\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  +\sum_{1\leq k\leq\nu}\left\{  \left(  D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  e^{i\mathbb{M}%
t}\right)  _{k}\bar{\eta}_{k}\left(  0\right)  +\left(  D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right]  e^{i\mathbb{M}%
t}\right)  _{k+\nu}\eta_{k}\left(  0\right)  \right\}  ,
\end{align}
which shows that derivatives wrt $\left\{  \mathbf{\bar{\eta},\eta}\right\}  $
are
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial\rho_{1jiH}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\bar{\eta}_{k}}  &
=\left(  D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right]  e^{i\mathbb{M}\tau}\right)  _{k}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{1jiH}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\eta_{k}}  &  =\left(
D^{1}\rho_{ji}\left[  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right]  e^{i\mathbb{M}\tau}\right)  _{k+\nu}.
\end{align}
As the time evolution is homogeneous the linear response functions eq.
$\left(  \ref{classres}\right)  $ become
\end{subequations}
\begin{equation}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \tau\right)  =\theta_{+}\left(  \tau\right)  \left\{
\rho_{1jiH}\left(  \tau\right)  ,\rho_{sr}\left(  0\right)  \right\}
_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}, \label{linearizedres}%
\end{equation}
so that
\begin{equation}
\delta\rho_{1ji}\left(  t\right)  =\sum_{r,s}\int_{0}^{t}\left\{  \rho
_{1Hji}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  ,\rho_{1sr}\left(  0\right)  \right\}
_{\mathbf{z}_{0}}f_{rs}\left(  t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}=\sum_{r,s}%
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}R_{ijrs}\left(  t-t^{\prime}\right)  f_{rs}\left(
t^{\prime}\right)  dt^{\prime}. \label{linresfordm}%
\end{equation}
Developing the response function eq.$\left(  \ref{linearizedres}\right)  $ we
obtain
\begin{align}
&  R_{ijrs}\left(  \tau\right)  =\nonumber\\
&  -i\theta_{+}\left(  \tau\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{2ji}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{\eta}}} &
\frac{\partial\rho_{2ji}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\eta}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{2sr}\left(  0\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{\eta}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{2sr}\left(  0\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\eta}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{2ji}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{\eta}}} &
\frac{\partial\rho_{2ji}\left(  \tau\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\eta}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \times\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\exp\left\{  -i\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  t\right\}
\end{align}
and
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{2sr}\left(  0\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{\eta}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{2sr}\left(  0\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\eta}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}
One can take the Laplace transform of eq. $\left(  \ref{linresfordm}\right)  $
to obtain
\begin{equation}
\delta\rho_{1ji}\left(  \zeta\right)  =\sum_{\leq1r,s\leq\nu}R_{ijrs}\left(
\zeta\right)  f_{rs}\left(  \zeta\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \zeta\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \times\nonumber\\
&  \left(  \zeta\mathbb{I}_{2\nu}-\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  ^{-1}\times\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & -i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
i\mathbf{C}^{-1}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \label{lap1}%
\end{align}
and $f_{rs}\left(  \zeta\right)  $ is the Laplace transform of $f_{rs}\left(
t\right)  .$ Eq. $\left(  \ref{lap1}\right)  $ can be equivalently written as
\begin{align}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \zeta\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \times\nonumber\\
&  \left(  \zeta\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right) \\
-\mathbf{\bar{C}}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  -\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  ^{-1}\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
\qquad\times\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  . \label{LinPhaseRes}%
\end{align}
which after inserting
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{1} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{1}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{1}\\
\mathbf{1} & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{1}\\
\mathbf{1} & \mathbf{0}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
after the first term on the RHS can be rearranged in terms of a hermitian
matrices as
\begin{align}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \zeta\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \times\nonumber\\
&  \left(  \zeta\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
& \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  -\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  ^{-1}\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
\qquad\times\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  . \label{hermres}%
\end{align}
Defining
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{C}  &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
& \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right) \nonumber\\
\mathfrak{D}  &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{align}
eq. $\left(  \ref{hermres}\right)  $ can be written as
\begin{equation}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \zeta\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\left(  \zeta\mathbb{I}_{2\nu
}-\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{DC}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\right)
^{-1}\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  , \label{resolv}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}
Then resolvent term in eq. $\left(  \ref{resolv}\right)  $ can be evaluated by
first noting that
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{DC}^{-\frac{1}{2}}  &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0} & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\\
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0} & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}%
^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right) \nonumber\\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0} & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\\
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0} & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}%
^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ^{\dagger}\nonumber\\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & -i\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{zz}}%
^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\\
-i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}%
}D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \equiv\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}%
\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & -i\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{zz}}%
^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\\
i\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\mathbf{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}} & \mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}D_{\mathbf{\bar
{z}z}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\mathbf{\bar{C}}^{-\frac{1}{2}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
Then using the spectral theorem to obtain $\left(  \text{see Appendix
\ref{Theorem}}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\eta_{j}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}%
_{j}^{\dagger}+\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}
;\qquad\eta_{j}\in\mathbb{R}, \label{spec1}%
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{v}_{j}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathbf{a}\\
\mathbf{b}%
\end{array}
\right)  ;\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\overline{\mathbf{b}}\\
\overline{\mathbf{a}}%
\end{array}
\right)  ;\quad\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger}\mathbf{v}_{k}=\delta_{jk}.
\end{equation}
Thus
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{I}_{2\nu}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}%
_{j}^{\dagger}+\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger
}-\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}  . \label{spec2}%
\end{equation}
Using eqs. $\left(  \ref{spec1}\right)  $ and $\left(  \ref{spec2}\right)  $
we obtain
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{DC}^{-\frac{1}{2}}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\eta_{j}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}%
_{j}^{\dagger}-\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\eta_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\eta_{j+\nu}=-\eta_{j}.
\end{equation}
Hence
\begin{align}
\left(  \zeta\mathfrak{C}-\mathfrak{D}\right)   &  =\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}%
}\left(  \zeta\mathbb{I}_{2\nu}-\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{DC}%
^{-\frac{1}{2}}\right)  \mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}}=\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}%
}\left(  \zeta\sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger
}-\sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\eta_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}}\\
&  =\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\left(  \zeta
-\eta_{j}\right)  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\mathfrak{C}^{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{align}
leading to
\begin{equation}
\left(  \zeta\mathfrak{C}-\mathfrak{D}\right)  ^{-1}=\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac
{1}{2}}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\left(  \zeta-\eta_{j}\right)
^{-1}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger}\right)  \mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}%
{2}}%
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
R_{ijrs}\left(  \zeta\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}} & \frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }{\partial
\mathbf{\bar{z}}}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\left(
\zeta-\eta_{j}\right)  ^{-1}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{\bar{z}}}\\
\frac{\partial\rho_{ji}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }{\partial\mathbf{z}}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


If the critical point $\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  $ of $\mathcal{E}_{0}$ is a local minimum the rearranged Hessian
$\mathfrak{D}$ is positive definite and is explicitly given by in terms of the
coset generators as%
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D_{\mathbf{z\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{zz}}^{2}\mathcal{E}%
_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right) \\
D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}\bar{z}}}^{2}\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  & D_{\mathbf{\bar{z}z}}^{2}%
\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma
}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left\{  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}H_{0}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle  & \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  \right\vert H_{0}\mathbf{YY}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  \right\rangle \\
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y^{\dagger}Y^{\dagger}}H_{0}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle  & \overline{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}H_{0}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle }%
\end{array}
\right)  \right. \nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\left.  -\mathcal{E}_{0}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle  & \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{YY}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle \\
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y^{\dagger}Y^{\dagger}}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle  & \overline{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y^{\dagger}Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle }%
\end{array}
\right)  \right\}  .
\end{align}
The metric matrix $\mathfrak{C}$ is given by
\begin{align}
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{C}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
& \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -\mathbf{\bar{C}}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0}%
,\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\nonumber\\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left\{  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle  & \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & -\overline{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(
\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle }%
\end{array}
\right)  \right. \nonumber\\
&  \left.  -\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}%
_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  }\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\vert
\mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right\rangle
& \mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0} & \overline{\left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}%
_{0}\right)  \right\vert \mathbf{Y}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \left.  \Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\vert \mathbf{Y}^{\dagger}\Phi\left(  \mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)
\right\rangle }%
\end{array}
\right)  \right\}  .
\end{align}
and the transformed Hessian%
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{DC}^{-\frac{1}{2}}  &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\\
\overline{\mathbf{B}} & \overline{\mathbf{A}}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\eta_{j}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}%
_{j}^{\dagger}-\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq2\nu}\eta_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}_{j}^{\dagger
}=\mathbf{V\eta V}^{\dagger}\nonumber\\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\eta_{j}\left\{  \mathbf{v}_{j}\mathbf{v}%
_{j}^{\dagger}+\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{v}_{j+\nu}^{\dagger}\right\}
\nonumber\\
&  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left\{  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\left(  \sigma_{j}-\mathcal{E}\left(
\mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \right)
\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{\Delta}^{\frac{1}{2}}\left\{
\mathbf{y}_{j}\mathbf{y}_{j}^{\dagger}+\mathbf{y}_{j+\nu}\mathbf{y}_{j+\nu
}^{\dagger}\right\}  \mathfrak{\Delta}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}%
{2}\dagger}\right\} \nonumber\\
&  =\frac{1}{\mathcal{S}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma
}_{0}\right)  }\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathbb{I}_{\nu} & \mathbb{O}\\
\mathbb{O} & -\mathbb{I}_{\nu}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{\Delta}^{\frac{1}{2}}%
\mathbf{Y}\left(  \mathbf{\sigma}-\mathcal{E}\left(  \mathbf{\bar{\varsigma}%
}_{0},\mathbf{\varsigma}_{0}\right)  \mathbb{I}_{2\nu}\right)  \mathbf{Y}%
^{\dagger}\mathfrak{\Delta}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathfrak{C}^{-\frac{1}{2}\dagger}%
\end{align}


